/* 自动生成：python3 tools/build.py，勿手改。英文课程，中文仍以 content.md / data.js 为准。 */
window.SITE_DATA_EN = {"chapters": [{"id": "01", "title": "What is the crypto scene?", "emoji": "🌍", "intro": "Meet chains, coins, wallets, and exchanges", "tags": ["Basics"], "tip": "Note: Look at what it is and why it exists before you decide to take part.", "sections": [{"title": "What is the crypto scene?", "html": "<p><strong>The crypto scene is the market and community around cryptocurrency, and buying coins is only one way to take part.</strong></p>\n<p>\"Crypto scene\" is an everyday way of talking about the market and community around cryptocurrency, blockchains, and related apps.<br />\nSome people trade, some build apps, and some study the technology, payments, games, digital collectibles, and on-chain finance.</p>\n<p>Buying coins is only one way to take part.<br />\nYou can also study first, watch projects, and try a test network, without putting in real money.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The crypto scene is a market and a community. Buying coins is only one way to take part.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Buying is not the only way. You can also learn, watch projects, and try a testnet before you put in real money.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is buying coins the only way to take part in the crypto scene?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If you do not buy, you are not taking part</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Learning and watching also count as taking part</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only building an app yourself counts</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The crypto scene is the market and community around cryptocurrency, and buying coins is only one way to take part. \"Crypto scene\" is an everyday way of talking about the market and community around cryptocurrency, blockchains, and related apps. Some people trade, some build apps, and some study the technology, payments, games, digital collectibles, and on-chain finance. Buying coins is only one way to take part. You can also study first, watch projects, and try a test network, without putting in real money. Think it through Is buying coins the only way to take part in the crypto scene? A. Yes. If you do not buy, you are not taking part B. No. Learning and watching also count as taking part C. Only building an app yourself counts"}, {"title": "What is a blockchain?", "html": "<p><strong>A blockchain is a public ledger that many people keep together, and that is hard for one person to quietly change.</strong></p>\n<p>You can think of a blockchain as a record system maintained by more than one party.<br />\nTransactions are confirmed by the rules and written into the record. Participants agree on the record through a consensus mechanism.</p>\n<p>One important property is that it resists tampering.<br />\nWhen the network is running normally, and its consensus and security assumptions hold, a record that is already well confirmed cannot be changed on a whim by one person.</p>\n<p>A record that resists tampering does not mean the information that was entered, a project's marketing, or the other side of a trade is true.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A record that is hard to change only means a confirmed record is hard for one person to quietly rewrite. What was written can still be untrue.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Resisting tampering is not the same as the content being true. A project's marketing and the other side of a trade still have to be checked on their own.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If a record is hard to change in secret, does that mean what was written is definitely true?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Once it is on a blockchain, it is true</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Resisting tampering is not the same as the content being true</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only a record kept by one company can be true</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A blockchain is a public ledger that many people keep together, and that is hard for one person to quietly change. You can think of a blockchain as a record system maintained by more than one party. Transactions are confirmed by the rules and written into the record. Participants agree on the record through a consensus mechanism. One important property is that it resists tampering. When the network is running normally, and its consensus and security assumptions hold, a record that is already well confirmed cannot be changed on a whim by one person. A record that resists tampering does not mean the information that was entered, a project's marketing, or the other side of a trade is true. Think it through If a record is hard to change in secret, does that mean what was written is definitely true? A. Yes. Once it is on a blockchain, it is true B. No. Resisting tampering is not the same as the content being true C. Only a record kept by one company can be true"}, {"title": "What is a cryptocurrency?", "html": "<p><strong>A cryptocurrency is a digital asset on a chain; there are many kinds, and their risks differ.</strong></p>\n<p>Cryptocurrencies are digital assets managed with cryptography and the rules of a network.<br />\nUses, how they are issued, and risks differ a lot. Do not treat them all as the same kind of investment.</p>\n<p>BTC is the native asset of the Bitcoin network.<br />\nETH is the native asset of the Ethereum network, and it can be used to pay network fees, among other things.<br />\nMany tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. BTC is the native asset of the Bitcoin network. Many tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists. Sharing the word coin does not make them the same thing.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Sharing the word coin does not mean the same issuance or the same risk. BTC belongs to the Bitcoin network itself. Many tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Are BTC and a token issued by a contract the same kind of thing?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If the name says coin, they are the same</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. BTC is the Bitcoin network's native asset, and many tokens are issued by contracts</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. They are the same only when the price is the same</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A cryptocurrency is a digital asset on a chain; there are many kinds, and their risks differ. Cryptocurrencies are digital assets managed with cryptography and the rules of a network. Uses, how they are issued, and risks differ a lot. Do not treat them all as the same kind of investment. BTC is the native asset of the Bitcoin network. ETH is the native asset of the Ethereum network, and it can be used to pay network fees, among other things. Many tokens are issued by contracts on a chain that already exists. Think it through Are BTC and a token issued by a contract the same kind of thing? A. Yes. If the name says coin, they are the same B. No. BTC is the Bitcoin network's native asset, and many tokens are issued by contracts C. They are the same only when the price is the same"}, {"title": "Where BTC came from", "html": "<p><strong>Bitcoin was the first cryptocurrency; it started running in 2009, and its supply is capped at about 21 million coins.</strong></p>\n<p>October 31, 2008<br />\nAn author using the name \"Satoshi Nakamoto\" published the Bitcoin white paper :<br />\n《Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System》<br />\nThe usual Chinese translation is \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System\"</p>\n<p>The question in the white paper is direct: can two people pay each other online without relying on a financial institution to stop the same money from being spent twice?<br />\nIt combines digital signatures, proof of work, and network consensus, and designs a peer-to-peer electronic cash system.</p>\n<p>On January 3, 2009, the Bitcoin genesis block was created, and the Bitcoin network started running.<br />\nSatoshi Nakamoto's real identity has never been confirmed in a way people agree on.</p>\n<p>Under the current consensus rules, the issuance cap for BTC is about 21 million coins.<br />\nIt is not issued by a company whenever the company wants more. It is produced gradually under the network's rules.<br />\nScarcity is one of its properties. It does not guarantee that the price only rises.</p>\n<p>📄 Want to read the white paper? Start from the official bitcoin.org pages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>English PDF: <a class=\"ext\" href=\"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf\">https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf</a><span class=\"ext-meta\"><span class=\"domain\">🌐 bitcoin.org</span><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></span></li>\n<li>Other languages: <a class=\"ext\" href=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\">https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper</a><span class=\"ext-meta\"><span class=\"domain\">🌐 bitcoin.org</span><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. Under the current consensus rules, the cap is about 21 million. That is an issuance rule. It does not guarantee that the price only rises.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. BTC is not printed by a company on demand. The cap is about 21 million, and scarcity does not mean the price only rises.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Under the current rules, about how many BTC can be issued at most?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. About 21 million, and the price is not guaranteed to only rise</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. A company prints more when demand rises</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. There is no cap. It can be issued without limit</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Bitcoin was the first cryptocurrency; it started running in 2009, and its supply is capped at about 21 million coins. October 31, 2008 An author using the name \"Satoshi Nakamoto\" published the Bitcoin white paper : 《Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System》 The usual Chinese translation is \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System\" The question in the white paper is direct: can two people pay each other online without relying on a financial institution to stop the same money from being spent twice? It combines digital signatures, proof of work, and network consensus, and designs a peer-to-peer electronic cash system. On January 3, 2009, the Bitcoin genesis block was created, and the Bitcoin network started running. Satoshi Nakamoto's real identity has never been confirmed in a way people agree on. Under the current consensus rules, the issuance cap for BTC is about 21 million coins. It is not issued by a company whenever the company wants more. It is produced gradually under the network's rules. Scarcity is one of its properties. It does not guarantee that the price only rises. 📄 Want to read the white paper? Start from the official bitcoin.org pages: English PDF: https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf 🌐 bitcoin.org ⏳ Not checked yet Other languages: https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper 🌐 bitcoin.org ⏳ Not checked yet Think it through Under the current rules, about how many BTC can be issued at most? A. About 21 million, and the price is not guaranteed to only rise B. A company prints more when demand rises C. There is no cap. It can be issued without limit"}, {"title": "Coin or token?", "html": "<p><strong>A native coin belongs to a chain itself, and a token is issued on someone else's chain.</strong></p>\n<p>A common distinction: a native coin belongs to a network itself.<br />\nA token is issued on a network that already exists.<br />\nETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum are the example.</p>\n<p>In everyday talk, people often call all of them coins.<br />\nThe same name does not mean they were issued the same way.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. ETH belongs to the Ethereum network itself. An ERC-20 token is issued on that existing network.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Calling them all coins is only a way of speaking. ETH is the native coin. An ERC-20 token is not the chain itself.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is the main difference between ETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. There is no difference. Being on Ethereum makes them the same</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. ETH belongs to the network itself. An ERC-20 token is issued on a network that already exists</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. ERC-20 is Ethereum's native coin</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A native coin belongs to a chain itself, and a token is issued on someone else's chain. A common distinction: a native coin belongs to a network itself. A token is issued on a network that already exists. ETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum are the example. In everyday talk, people often call all of them coins. The same name does not mean they were issued the same way. Think it through What is the main difference between ETH and an ERC-20 token on Ethereum? A. There is no difference. Being on Ethereum makes them the same B. ETH belongs to the network itself. An ERC-20 token is issued on a network that already exists C. ERC-20 is Ethereum's native coin"}, {"title": "What is a public chain?", "html": "<p><strong>A public chain is a blockchain anyone can use, and you cannot send between chains as if they were the same place.</strong></p>\n<p>A public chain is usually an open blockchain network, for example Bitcoin, Ethereum, TRON, BNB Chain, and Solana.<br />\nFees, speed, apps, and security assumptions differ from chain to chain.</p>\n<p>You can think of different networks as different transport systems.<br />\nAn address that looks the same does not mean the destination and the asset are compatible.<br />\nA transfer needs the recipient to explicitly support that network.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A similar-looking address does not mean the network and the asset are compatible. The recipient has to explicitly support that network.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not send just because the address looks similar, or because both sides are called public chains. Confirm the network the recipient supports first.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If an address looks the same, can you send it straight onto another chain?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If the address looks the same, you can send</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not by default. The recipient has to explicitly support that network</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If both are called public chains, they can send to each other</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A public chain is a blockchain anyone can use, and you cannot send between chains as if they were the same place. A public chain is usually an open blockchain network, for example Bitcoin, Ethereum, TRON, BNB Chain, and Solana. Fees, speed, apps, and security assumptions differ from chain to chain. You can think of different networks as different transport systems. An address that looks the same does not mean the destination and the asset are compatible. A transfer needs the recipient to explicitly support that network. Think it through If an address looks the same, can you send it straight onto another chain? A. Yes. If the address looks the same, you can send B. Not by default. The recipient has to explicitly support that network C. If both are called public chains, they can send to each other"}, {"title": "What is Web3?", "html": "<p><strong>Web3 means internet apps you sign in to with a wallet and use on a chain.</strong></p>\n<p>Web3 is often used for internet apps built with blockchains, wallets, and smart contracts.<br />\nA user can manage digital assets, sign actions, and use on-chain apps through a wallet.</p>\n<p>But a Web3 label does not mean the project is actually decentralized.<br />\nIt also does not mean the app is usable or safe.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Web3 is only a label. It does not guarantee decentralization, and it does not guarantee that something is usable or safe.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A Web3 label, or a wallet sign-in, does not by itself prove safety or decentralization.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If an app is labeled Web3, does that mean it is already decentralized and safe?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. The label is the proof</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The label does not guarantee decentralization, usability, or safety</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If you can sign in with a wallet, it is definitely safe</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Web3 means internet apps you sign in to with a wallet and use on a chain. Web3 is often used for internet apps built with blockchains, wallets, and smart contracts. A user can manage digital assets, sign actions, and use on-chain apps through a wallet. But a Web3 label does not mean the project is actually decentralized. It also does not mean the app is usable or safe. Think it through If an app is labeled Web3, does that mean it is already decentralized and safe? A. Yes. The label is the proof B. No. The label does not guarantee decentralization, usability, or safety C. If you can sign in with a wallet, it is definitely safe"}, {"title": "What is a smart contract?", "html": "<p><strong>A smart contract is a program on a chain that runs by its code, and if the code has a bug, things can go wrong.</strong></p>\n<p>A smart contract is a program deployed on a blockchain that executes by its code.<br />\nSwaps, lending, and token issuance are often done by one.</p>\n<p>Code can have bugs, and it can also be given admin permissions.<br />\nDo not assume it is reliable just because it is called smart.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. It is a program that runs by its code. The code can have bugs, and it may also have admin permissions.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Calling it smart does not mean it cannot fail. A swap contract and other contracts can both have bugs or admin permissions.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Can a smart contract be treated as something that will not go wrong?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, because it is called smart</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The code can have bugs, and it may also have admin permissions</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only swap contracts can go wrong</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A smart contract is a program on a chain that runs by its code, and if the code has a bug, things can go wrong. A smart contract is a program deployed on a blockchain that executes by its code. Swaps, lending, and token issuance are often done by one. Code can have bugs, and it can also be given admin permissions. Do not assume it is reliable just because it is called smart. Think it through Can a smart contract be treated as something that will not go wrong? A. Yes, because it is called smart B. No. The code can have bugs, and it may also have admin permissions C. Only swap contracts can go wrong"}, {"title": "What is a stablecoin?", "html": "<p><strong>A stablecoin aims to stay 1:1 with the dollar, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose that peg.</strong></p>\n<p>A stablecoin aims to track a reference value, often 1 US dollar, for example USDT and USDC.<br />\nDifferent stablecoins may use fiat reserves, crypto collateral, or other mechanisms.</p>\n<p>A stablecoin can be used for pricing, transfers, and settling trades.<br />\nIt is not a bank deposit, and it is not guaranteed to stay redeemable at 1 dollar.<br />\nYou need to understand the reserves, the redemption terms, freeze permissions, and the risk of losing the peg.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. It aims to track a reference value, such as 1 dollar. It is not a bank deposit, and it can lose the peg.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. USD in the name does not mean it is a bank deposit, or that it will always equal 1 dollar.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Is a stablecoin a bank deposit, and is it guaranteed to always equal 1 US dollar?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, so it can be treated as a deposit</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. It aims to track a reference value, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose the peg</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the name contains USD, it always equals 1 dollar</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A stablecoin aims to stay 1:1 with the dollar, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose that peg. A stablecoin aims to track a reference value, often 1 US dollar, for example USDT and USDC. Different stablecoins may use fiat reserves, crypto collateral, or other mechanisms. A stablecoin can be used for pricing, transfers, and settling trades. It is not a bank deposit, and it is not guaranteed to stay redeemable at 1 dollar. You need to understand the reserves, the redemption terms, freeze permissions, and the risk of losing the peg. Think it through Is a stablecoin a bank deposit, and is it guaranteed to always equal 1 US dollar? A. Yes, so it can be treated as a deposit B. No. It aims to track a reference value, but it is not a bank deposit, and it can lose the peg C. If the name contains USD, it always equals 1 dollar"}, {"title": "One table of how the pieces fit", "html": "<p><strong>One table: what the chain, the coin, the wallet, the exchange, and the app each do.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Main job</th>\n<th>Simple example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blockchain</td>\n<td>Records and carries out network activity</td>\n<td>Ethereum, TRON</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coin / token</td>\n<td>A digital asset inside a network</td>\n<td>BTC, ETH, USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wallet</td>\n<td>Manages the account and signs actions</td>\n<td>Software wallet, hardware wallet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exchange</td>\n<td>Buys, sells, or swaps assets</td>\n<td>CEX, DEX</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DApp</td>\n<td>An application on a blockchain</td>\n<td>Swap, lending, or game apps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A wallet manages the account and the signing.<br />\nIt does not keep the blockchain's books, and it does not guarantee the price on an exchange.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A wallet mainly manages the account, and it is what you use to sign actions.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Keeping the books is the blockchain network's job. An exchange price is not guaranteed by the wallet. The wallet manages the account and the signing.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What does a wallet mainly do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Keep the books in place of the blockchain</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Manage the account, and sign actions</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Guarantee that the price on an exchange will not change</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "One table: what the chain, the coin, the wallet, the exchange, and the app each do. Name Main job Simple example Blockchain Records and carries out network activity Ethereum, TRON Coin / token A digital asset inside a network BTC, ETH, USDT Wallet Manages the account and signs actions Software wallet, hardware wallet Exchange Buys, sells, or swaps assets CEX, DEX DApp An application on a blockchain Swap, lending, or game apps A wallet manages the account and the signing. It does not keep the blockchain's books, and it does not guarantee the price on an exchange. Think it through What does a wallet mainly do? A. Keep the books in place of the blockchain B. Manage the account, and sign actions C. Guarantee that the price on an exchange will not change"}, {"title": "Why do prices move?", "html": "<p><strong>The price is set by buyers and sellers; a rise does not mean it is worth more, and a drop does not mean it is cheaper.</strong></p>\n<p>The trading price is formed by buying and selling in the market.<br />\nIt can be moved by demand, liquidity, expectations, changes in supply, leverage, the broader economy, and news.</p>\n<p>A rising price does not necessarily mean the project is more valuable.<br />\nA falling price does not automatically prove that it is cheap.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The price is formed by buying and selling. A rise does not mean the project is more valuable, and a drop does not automatically mean it is cheap.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A price move by itself cannot prove that a project is more valuable, or that it is already cheap.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>If the price rises, does that mean the project is more valuable?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If it rose, it is more valuable</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. The price is formed by buying and selling, and a rise does not mean the project is more valuable</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only a drop can show that it has no value</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The price is set by buyers and sellers; a rise does not mean it is worth more, and a drop does not mean it is cheaper. The trading price is formed by buying and selling in the market. It can be moved by demand, liquidity, expectations, changes in supply, leverage, the broader economy, and news. A rising price does not necessarily mean the project is more valuable. A falling price does not automatically prove that it is cheap. Note: Look at what it is and why it exists before you decide to take part. Think it through If the price rises, does that mean the project is more valuable? A. Yes. If it rose, it is more valuable B. Not necessarily. The price is formed by buying and selling, and a rise does not mean the project is more valuable C. Only a drop can show that it has no value"}]}, {"id": "02", "title": "Primary and secondary markets", "emoji": "🌱", "intro": "Are you joining an issuance, or trading coins already out?", "tags": ["Basics"], "tip": "Note: First say which asset you are buying, and who issued it, before you talk about primary or secondary.", "sections": [{"title": "Primary market: issuance and early funding", "html": "<p><strong>The primary market is the subscription when a coin is first issued, and early does not mean easier money.</strong></p>\n<p>The primary market usually means the first issuance, the funding, or the subscription.<br />\nIn crypto, common forms include private sales, public sales, token issuances, and some Launchpad events.<br />\nThe exact category depends on what the participant is buying, who the seller is, and how the deal is structured.</p>\n<p>Fictional teaching example: a project opens a token subscription to participants for the first time, and the participants receive the tokens from the issuer.<br />\nThat is usually a primary issuance.<br />\nIt is not a real project, and it does not mean taking part this way will make money.</p>\n<p>Early participation can mean a longer lockup, less public information, and a harder exit.<br />\n\"Early\" only means earlier in time. It does not mean easier money.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Early only means earlier in time. It can also mean a longer lockup, less information, and a harder exit.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Early does not mean easier money. A private sale is not more profitable just because it is earlier.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Does \"early\" mean it is easier to make money?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. The earlier it is, the easier it is to profit</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Early only means earlier in time, and the exit can be harder</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only a private sale is easier money because it is earlier</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The primary market is the subscription when a coin is first issued, and early does not mean easier money. The primary market usually means the first issuance, the funding, or the subscription. In crypto, common forms include private sales, public sales, token issuances, and some Launchpad events. The exact category depends on what the participant is buying, who the seller is, and how the deal is structured. Fictional teaching example: a project opens a token subscription to participants for the first time, and the participants receive the tokens from the issuer. That is usually a primary issuance. It is not a real project, and it does not mean taking part this way will make money. Early participation can mean a longer lockup, less public information, and a harder exit. \"Early\" only means earlier in time. It does not mean easier money. Think it through Does \"early\" mean it is easier to make money? A. Yes. The earlier it is, the easier it is to profit B. No. Early only means earlier in time, and the exit can be harder C. Only a private sale is easier money because it is earlier"}, {"title": "Secondary market: trading coins already issued", "html": "<p><strong>The secondary market is buying and selling coins that someone else already holds.</strong></p>\n<p>The secondary market usually means assets that have already been issued, moving between holders.<br />\nBuying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange, or swapping an already issued token on a DEX, is generally a secondary trade.</p>\n<p>So primary / secondary describes issuance and later circulation.<br />\nCEX / DEX describes the venue and the mechanism.<br />\nThey are not the same classification.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Buying BTC that someone else already holds is generally a secondary trade. It does not become a primary issuance just because it happens on a CEX.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Primary is the issuance step. Buying already issued BTC on an exchange is generally a secondary trade. CEX names the venue, not primary or secondary.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Buying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange is generally which kind?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. A primary issuance</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. A secondary trade</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Unrelated to primary or secondary. It only matters whether it is a CEX</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "The secondary market is buying and selling coins that someone else already holds. The secondary market usually means assets that have already been issued, moving between holders. Buying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange, or swapping an already issued token on a DEX, is generally a secondary trade. So primary / secondary describes issuance and later circulation. CEX / DEX describes the venue and the mechanism. They are not the same classification. Think it through Buying BTC that someone else is selling on an exchange is generally which kind? A. A primary issuance B. A secondary trade C. Unrelated to primary or secondary. It only matters whether it is a CEX"}, {"title": "Common ways to take part early", "html": "<p><strong>Private sales, IDOs, airdrops, and testnets are all early ways to take part, and each has its own trap.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Way</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Check carefully</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Private sale</td>\n<td>Raising money from specific participants</td>\n<td>Eligibility, the contract, the price, the lockup, and delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public sale</td>\n<td>A raise open to people who qualify</td>\n<td>Regional limits, subscription rules, and the issuance plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IDO</td>\n<td>An issuance organized through a decentralized platform</td>\n<td>Whether it is genuine, the contract, liquidity, and the allocation rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Launchpad</td>\n<td>A project issuance organized by a platform</td>\n<td>Platform rules, the cost of taking part, the allocation, and the unlock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airdrop</td>\n<td>Assets allocated to addresses or users who qualify</td>\n<td>The conditions, the cost of claiming, and approval risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testnet</td>\n<td>Trying a product on a test network</td>\n<td>Test assets are usually not real assets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Points campaign</td>\n<td>A points system that records user activity</td>\n<td>Whether points can be redeemed, and whether the rules can change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>An airdrop, points, and a testnet are not automatically a primary investment.<br />\nThey also do not guarantee a future token or a reward.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A testnet is for trying the product. Test assets are usually not real assets.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Test assets usually cannot be treated as real assets. A high points score does not mean the test assets have become real assets.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Are test assets on a testnet usually the same as real assets?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Once the test passes, you can use them as real assets</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Usually not</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the points are high enough, they are real assets</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Private sales, IDOs, airdrops, and testnets are all early ways to take part, and each has its own trap. Way Meaning Check carefully Private sale Raising money from specific participants Eligibility, the contract, the price, the lockup, and delivery Public sale A raise open to people who qualify Regional limits, subscription rules, and the issuance plan IDO An issuance organized through a decentralized platform Whether it is genuine, the contract, liquidity, and the allocation rules Launchpad A project issuance organized by a platform Platform rules, the cost of taking part, the allocation, and the unlock Airdrop Assets allocated to addresses or users who qualify The conditions, the cost of claiming, and approval risk Testnet Trying a product on a test network Test assets are usually not real assets Points campaign A points system that records user activity Whether points can be redeemed, and whether the rules can change An airdrop, points, and a testnet are not automatically a primary investment. They also do not guarantee a future token or a reward. Think it through Are test assets on a testnet usually the same as real assets? A. Yes. Once the test passes, you can use them as real assets B. Usually not C. If the points are high enough, they are real assets"}, {"title": "What are lockups and unlocks?", "html": "<p><strong>A lockup means you cannot sell for a while, and after the unlock, more of the coin can be sold.</strong></p>\n<p>A lockup means the asset cannot be transferred or withdrawn for a time, under certain conditions.<br />\nAn unlock means the restriction is lifted on a schedule.<br />\nAn early subscription may be delivered in batches. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell immediately.</p>\n<p>An unlock changes the supply that can circulate.<br />\nThe actual effect on price still depends on what holders do, on demand, and on market liquidity.<br />\nAn unlock does not mean the price will definitely rise.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. During a lockup you may be unable to transfer for a while. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell right away.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell immediately. An unlock increases the amount that can be sold, but it does not guarantee a higher price.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Once the subscription is finished, can you sell immediately?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Once you have paid, you can sell</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Not necessarily. During a lockup you may be unable to transfer for a while</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. After the unlock, the price definitely rises</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A lockup means you cannot sell for a while, and after the unlock, more of the coin can be sold. A lockup means the asset cannot be transferred or withdrawn for a time, under certain conditions. An unlock means the restriction is lifted on a schedule. An early subscription may be delivered in batches. Finishing the subscription does not mean you can sell immediately. An unlock changes the supply that can circulate. The actual effect on price still depends on what holders do, on demand, and on market liquidity. An unlock does not mean the price will definitely rise. Think it through Once the subscription is finished, can you sell immediately? A. Yes. Once you have paid, you can sell B. Not necessarily. During a lockup you may be unable to transfer for a while C. After the unlock, the price definitely rises"}, {"title": "CEX and DEX", "html": "<p><strong>CEX / DEX is about where you trade, and it is not the same thing as a primary or secondary market.</strong></p>\n<p>CEX and DEX classify the venue. They are not the primary / secondary distinction.<br />\nThe difference is in <a href=\"#/c/05\">Chapter 05, &quot;How is a CEX different from a DEX?&quot;</a>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. CEX and DEX distinguish the venue and the mechanism. Primary and secondary distinguish issuance and later circulation.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. CEX / DEX is not primary versus secondary, and it is not coin versus token. It says where you trade, and by what mechanism.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What do the words CEX and DEX mainly distinguish?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. The primary market and the secondary market</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The venue and the mechanism</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. A native coin and a token</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "CEX / DEX is about where you trade, and it is not the same thing as a primary or secondary market. CEX and DEX classify the venue. They are not the primary / secondary distinction. The difference is in Chapter 05, \"How is a CEX different from a DEX?\" . Note: First say which asset you are buying, and who issued it, before you talk about primary or secondary. Think it through What do the words CEX and DEX mainly distinguish? A. The primary market and the secondary market B. The venue and the mechanism C. A native coin and a token"}]}, {"id": "03", "title": "What to know before you buy", "emoji": "📚", "intro": "Price, market cap, liquidity, and fees", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "Trading pairs", "html": "<p><strong>BTC/USDT means you buy BTC with USDT. You do not have to buy a whole coin.</strong></p>\n<p>BTC/USDT means BTC is priced in USDT. If the price is 60,000, 1 BTC corresponds to about 60,000 USDT. Buying BTC/USDT usually means spending USDT to buy BTC. Selling is the reverse.</p>\n<p>You do not need a whole BTC. Many assets can be bought in fractions, but platforms have a minimum order size.</p>", "text": "BTC/USDT means you buy BTC with USDT. You do not have to buy a whole coin. BTC/USDT means BTC is priced in USDT. If the price is 60,000, 1 BTC corresponds to about 60,000 USDT. Buying BTC/USDT usually means spending USDT to buy BTC. Selling is the reverse. You do not need a whole BTC. Many assets can be bought in fractions, but platforms have a minimum order size."}, {"title": "Price, market cap, and FDV", "html": "<p><strong>A low unit price is not \"cheap\". Look at market cap and total supply.</strong></p>\n<p>Price: the market price of one unit.<br />\nCirculating market cap: commonly price × circulating supply.<br />\nFDV: fully diluted valuation, commonly price × total supply or max supply. Data sites do not all use the same definition.</p>\n<p>Suppose a coin is priced at 0.01 dollar and 10 billion are circulating. Circulating market cap is about 100 million dollars. Another coin is 100 dollars each, with 100,000 circulating. Market cap is about 10 million dollars.</p>\n<p>So a low unit price is not a low valuation. Market cap is also not the total cash everyone actually put in.</p>", "text": "A low unit price is not \"cheap\". Look at market cap and total supply. Price: the market price of one unit. Circulating market cap: commonly price × circulating supply. FDV: fully diluted valuation, commonly price × total supply or max supply. Data sites do not all use the same definition. Suppose a coin is priced at 0.01 dollar and 10 billion are circulating. Circulating market cap is about 100 million dollars. Another coin is 100 dollars each, with 100,000 circulating. Market cap is about 10 million dollars. So a low unit price is not a low valuation. Market cap is also not the total cash everyone actually put in."}, {"title": "Supply", "html": "<p><strong>Before you stare at the number, ask whether more can be issued, and when unlocks happen.</strong></p>\n<p>Circulating supply is the amount already in circulation under a given definition. Total supply is usually what has been issued, minus some burned amount. Max supply is the ceiling the rules allow. Not every asset has a fixed ceiling.</p>\n<p>Whether supply can increase, who has that power, and when the team and investors unlock is more useful than one number on its own.</p>", "text": "Before you stare at the number, ask whether more can be issued, and when unlocks happen. Circulating supply is the amount already in circulation under a given definition. Total supply is usually what has been issued, minus some burned amount. Max supply is the ceiling the rules allow. Not every asset has a fixed ceiling. Whether supply can increase, who has that power, and when the team and investors unlock is more useful than one number on its own."}, {"title": "Liquidity and volume", "html": "<p><strong>When liquidity is good, your own trade is less likely to shove the price around.</strong></p>\n<p>Liquidity is how easily you can buy or sell without moving the price much. Volume is how much traded over a period of time, in amount or in value.</p>\n<p>High volume does not mean every moment has deep liquidity. Volume can also be faked.</p>", "text": "When liquidity is good, your own trade is less likely to shove the price around. Liquidity is how easily you can buy or sell without moving the price much. Volume is how much traded over a period of time, in amount or in value. High volume does not mean every moment has deep liquidity. Volume can also be faked."}, {"title": "Spread, price impact, and slippage", "html": "<p><strong>The price on the screen is not always the price you get.</strong></p>\n<p>The spread is the gap between the best ask and the best bid.<br />\nPrice impact is how much your own order moves the fill price. It shows up when the book is thin or the pool is small.<br />\nSlippage is the gap between the price you expected and the price you got. It can come from the market moving, from depth, and from other factors.</p>\n<p>Example: the screen says about 1 dollar. That does not mean a very large market order will all fill at 1 dollar.</p>", "text": "The price on the screen is not always the price you get. The spread is the gap between the best ask and the best bid. Price impact is how much your own order moves the fill price. It shows up when the book is thin or the pool is small. Slippage is the gap between the price you expected and the price you got. It can come from the market moving, from depth, and from other factors. Example: the screen says about 1 dollar. That does not mean a very large market order will all fill at 1 dollar."}, {"title": "Trading fees and network fees", "html": "<p><strong>To know the cost, add the trading fee, the spread, slippage, and the withdrawal fee.</strong></p>\n<p>A trading fee is charged by the platform or the protocol. An on-chain network fee usually pays for executing the transaction and for network resources. An exchange withdrawal fee is not necessarily the chain's real cost at that moment.</p>\n<p>Look at them together: trading fee, spread, slippage, withdrawal fee, network fee, exchange rate, and payment-channel fees.</p>\n<h3>What one round trip actually costs</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cost</th>\n<th>What beginners skip</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Buying the coin</td>\n<td>The payment channel's quote, the exchange rate, and the spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The trade</td>\n<td>Fees and slippage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Withdrawing the coin</td>\n<td>The platform withdrawal fee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On-chain actions</td>\n<td>Network fees for approvals, swaps, and similar steps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A contract position</td>\n<td>Funding fees and similar costs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Example (round numbers chosen so the arithmetic is easy, not any platform's real fees):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Assumed cost</th>\n<th>Left</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ready to buy 100 USDT of a coin</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>100 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quote and spread when buying</td>\n<td>about 1 USDT</td>\n<td>99 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trading fee and slippage</td>\n<td>about 0.5 USDT</td>\n<td>98.5 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Withdraw to your own wallet</td>\n<td>withdrawal fee about 1 USDT</td>\n<td>97.5 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approve and swap on-chain</td>\n<td>network fee about 1.5 USDT</td>\n<td>96 USDT</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The coin price barely moved, and the balance is down about 4%. That is why \"the price hardly moved, but I have less\": every step takes a little. If you also open a contract, funding while you hold keeps changing the balance. The smaller the amount and the more steps you take, the larger the cost as a share of the money.</p>", "text": "To know the cost, add the trading fee, the spread, slippage, and the withdrawal fee. A trading fee is charged by the platform or the protocol. An on-chain network fee usually pays for executing the transaction and for network resources. An exchange withdrawal fee is not necessarily the chain's real cost at that moment. Look at them together: trading fee, spread, slippage, withdrawal fee, network fee, exchange rate, and payment-channel fees. What one round trip actually costs Cost What beginners skip Buying the coin The payment channel's quote, the exchange rate, and the spread The trade Fees and slippage Withdrawing the coin The platform withdrawal fee On-chain actions Network fees for approvals, swaps, and similar steps A contract position Funding fees and similar costs Example (round numbers chosen so the arithmetic is easy, not any platform's real fees): Step Assumed cost Left Ready to buy 100 USDT of a coin — 100 USDT Quote and spread when buying about 1 USDT 99 USDT Trading fee and slippage about 0.5 USDT 98.5 USDT Withdraw to your own wallet withdrawal fee about 1 USDT 97.5 USDT Approve and swap on-chain network fee about 1.5 USDT 96 USDT The coin price barely moved, and the balance is down about 4%. That is why \"the price hardly moved, but I have less\": every step takes a little. If you also open a contract, funding while you hold keeps changing the balance. The smaller the amount and the more steps you take, the larger the cost as a share of the money."}, {"title": "How do profit and loss work?", "html": "<p><strong>After a 50% drop, you need a 100% rise to get back to the start.</strong></p>\n<p>A simple spot example: you put in 100 USDT and sell for 110 USDT. Ignoring fees, profit is 10 USDT, a 10% return.</p>\n<p>After a 50% drop you need a 100% rise to get back to the old price: 100 → 50 → 100.</p>\n<p>Several buys, partial sells, contracts, and prices quoted in more than one coin need a fuller calculation. The last price alone is not enough.</p>", "text": "After a 50% drop, you need a 100% rise to get back to the start. A simple spot example: you put in 100 USDT and sell for 110 USDT. Ignoring fees, profit is 10 USDT, a 10% return. After a 50% drop you need a 100% rise to get back to the old price: 100 → 50 → 100. Several buys, partial sells, contracts, and prices quoted in more than one coin need a fuller calculation. The last price alone is not enough."}, {"title": "Before you trade, you should be able to answer", "html": "<p><strong>If you cannot say what you are buying, or how much you can lose, do not buy yet.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>What am I buying? What coin is the price quoted in?</li>\n<li>Which fees do I pay? Can I sell or withdraw normally?</li>\n<li>Is there a lockup, extra issuance, an unlock, or admin power in the contract?</li>\n<li>What is the worst loss? Can I afford to lose this money entirely?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "If you cannot say what you are buying, or how much you can lose, do not buy yet. What am I buying? What coin is the price quoted in? Which fees do I pay? Can I sell or withdraw normally? Is there a lockup, extra issuance, an unlock, or admin power in the contract? What is the worst loss? Can I afford to lose this money entirely?"}]}, {"id": "04", "title": "A first buy, and moving money in and out", "emoji": "🛒", "intro": "From sign-up to buying, then selling and withdrawing", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "What are deposits and withdrawals?", "html": "<p><strong>A deposit puts money onto the platform. A withdrawal takes it out. The rules are not the same.</strong></p>\n<p>A deposit moves fiat or a digital asset onto the platform. A withdrawal moves funds out: withdrawing coins to a wallet, or selling coins and sending fiat to a bank.</p>\n<p>\"The coin withdrawal succeeded\" does not mean \"the fiat is already in the bank\". The rules, fees, and risks are different.</p>", "text": "A deposit puts money onto the platform. A withdrawal takes it out. The rules are not the same. A deposit moves fiat or a digital asset onto the platform. A withdrawal moves funds out: withdrawing coins to a wallet, or selling coins and sending fiat to a bank. \"The coin withdrawal succeeded\" does not mean \"the fiat is already in the bank\". The rules, fees, and risks are different."}, {"title": "Step 1: platform and region", "html": "<p><strong>When you sign up, check whether the platform serves your region. The sign-up page usually says.</strong></p>\n<p>One brand may be operated by different legal entities in different places. The products are not identical.</p>\n<p><strong>How to choose an exchange:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Look at the size of funds on the platform. That stock of user funds is one common reference.</li>\n<li>Look at proof of reserves. It can be a reference, but it is a snapshot at one moment. It does not guarantee the platform will not fail (see chapter 05).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you cannot use a platform, do not get around the limit with a fake identity or a false place of residence.</p>", "text": "When you sign up, check whether the platform serves your region. The sign-up page usually says. One brand may be operated by different legal entities in different places. The products are not identical. How to choose an exchange: Look at the size of funds on the platform. That stock of user funds is one common reference. Look at proof of reserves. It can be a reference, but it is a snapshot at one moment. It does not guarantee the platform will not fail (see chapter 05). If you cannot use a platform, do not get around the limit with a fake identity or a false place of residence."}, {"title": "Step 2: sign up and lock the account down", "html": "<p><strong>The first thing after sign-up: a strong password, plus two-factor authentication.</strong></p>\n<p>Use a strong password you do not reuse. Protect the sign-up email. Where the platform supports it, turn on a passkey, an authenticator, or another second factor. Keep the recovery method safe. Check devices and login history. Turn on withdrawal protection if it is available.</p>\n<p>KYC is identity verification. Submit documents only in the official app or on the official site after you have confirmed it. Do not send an ID card to a \"support agent\" who messaged you first.</p>", "text": "The first thing after sign-up: a strong password, plus two-factor authentication. Use a strong password you do not reuse. Protect the sign-up email. Where the platform supports it, turn on a passkey, an authenticator, or another second factor. Keep the recovery method safe. Check devices and login history. Turn on withdrawal protection if it is available. KYC is identity verification. Submit documents only in the official app or on the official site after you have confirmed it. Do not send an ID card to a \"support agent\" who messaged you first."}, {"title": "Step 3: how you will deposit", "html": "<p><strong>Before you deposit, read which banks are supported, the fees, and how long it takes.</strong></p>\n<p>Card or bank transfer: check supported banks, currency, who must be the payer, arrival time, and fees.<br />\nA third-party buy channel: check who the provider is, the quote, and the fees.<br />\nP2P: you trade with another user. Fiat and the digital asset settle through the platform's process.<br />\nDepositing crypto you already hold: pick an asset and a network both sides support, and fill in Memo / Tag if it is required.</p>", "text": "Before you deposit, read which banks are supported, the fees, and how long it takes. Card or bank transfer: check supported banks, currency, who must be the payer, arrival time, and fees. A third-party buy channel: check who the provider is, the quote, and the fees. P2P: you trade with another user. Fiat and the digital asset settle through the platform's process. Depositing crypto you already hold: pick an asset and a network both sides support, and fill in Memo / Tag if it is required."}, {"title": "P2P, in particular", "html": "<p><strong>In P2P, only your own account and a real arrival count. Do not trust a screenshot.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use an account in your own name, as the platform requires. Check the order and the payment details.</li>\n<li>Do not send funds to an off-platform address or a chat link the other person just sent.</li>\n<li>When you sell coins, the record that matters is the real arrival in your own bank. Do not rely only on a payment screenshot or a text message.</li>\n<li>If a third party pays, or someone asks you to split the order or change the process, pause and follow the platform's rules.</li>\n<li>If there is a dispute, use the platform's appeal and keep the chat, the order, and the payment proof.</li>\n<li>Platform custody does not remove the risk of fraud, chargebacks, a frozen bank account, or tainted funds.</li>\n</ul>", "text": "In P2P, only your own account and a real arrival count. Do not trust a screenshot. Use an account in your own name, as the platform requires. Check the order and the payment details. Do not send funds to an off-platform address or a chat link the other person just sent. When you sell coins, the record that matters is the real arrival in your own bank. Do not rely only on a payment screenshot or a text message. If a third party pays, or someone asks you to split the order or change the process, pause and follow the platform's rules. If there is a dispute, use the platform's appeal and keep the chat, the order, and the payment proof. Platform custody does not remove the risk of fraud, chargebacks, a frozen bank account, or tainted funds."}, {"title": "Step 4: buy a little spot", "html": "<p><strong>The first time, buy spot with a small amount. Learn the path.</strong></p>\n<p>Look at the pair and the order type. Check the asset you pay, the amount you expect to receive, and the fees. Afterward, check that the order filled and that the asset is in the right account.</p>\n<p>\"Instant convert\" and \"spot order\" may be quoted differently. Compare how much you actually receive, not only the advertised fee.</p>", "text": "The first time, buy spot with a small amount. Learn the path. Look at the pair and the order type. Check the asset you pay, the amount you expect to receive, and the fees. Afterward, check that the order filled and that the asset is in the right account. \"Instant convert\" and \"spot order\" may be quoted differently. Compare how much you actually receive, not only the advertised fee."}, {"title": "Step 5: selling and withdrawing", "html": "<p><strong>What you receive after a sale depends on the pair. Getting fiat back into a bank also depends on the cash-out channel.</strong></p>\n<p>A sale usually pays you the quote asset of the pair first. If you need fiat, use a fiat trade and withdrawal channel that your location allows. Check the receiving account, the fees, and the platform's limits.</p>\n<p>Keep trade records and records of where the funds came from. For local regulation, reporting, and tax, check official regulators, the tax authority, or a suitable professional.</p>\n<p>Note: The goal of a first try is to understand the process, not to prove you can catch a rally.</p>", "text": "What you receive after a sale depends on the pair. Getting fiat back into a bank also depends on the cash-out channel. A sale usually pays you the quote asset of the pair first. If you need fiat, use a fiat trade and withdrawal channel that your location allows. Check the receiving account, the fees, and the platform's limits. Keep trade records and records of where the funds came from. For local regulation, reporting, and tax, check official regulators, the tax authority, or a suitable professional. Note: The goal of a first try is to understand the process, not to prove you can catch a rally."}, {"title": "The accounts inside an exchange", "html": "<p><strong>Moving funds between accounts on the platform is not the same as an on-chain transfer.</strong></p>\n<p>Many exchanges split assets across accounts. Common ones:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Account / action</th>\n<th>What it is for</th>\n<th>Watch out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Funding account</td>\n<td>Deposits landing, P2P buys and sells, and withdrawals often use this</td>\n<td>On most platforms, coins kept here cannot be used directly for a spot or contract order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spot account</td>\n<td>Spot trades and instant convert</td>\n<td>To withdraw, you may need to move funds back to the funding account first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contract account</td>\n<td>Margin for contract trading</td>\n<td>Open positions can limit transfers out. Losses are taken from this balance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transfer between accounts</td>\n<td>Moving assets between accounts on the same platform</td>\n<td>Internal bookkeeping. It does not go on-chain, usually has no network fee, and arrives almost at once</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On-chain withdrawal</td>\n<td>Sending coins from the exchange to an external wallet or another platform's address</td>\n<td>This really goes on-chain. You choose a network, enter an address, pay a withdrawal fee, and cannot undo it after it arrives</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>If \"the balance disappeared\", check whether it is sitting in another account. If an order says \"insufficient balance\", you may need to move the asset into the account that order uses.</p>\n<p>Names and transfer rules differ by platform. Some platforms merge spot and contracts into a \"unified account\". Follow the app screen and the official explanation of the platform you use.</p>", "text": "Moving funds between accounts on the platform is not the same as an on-chain transfer. Many exchanges split assets across accounts. Common ones: Account / action What it is for Watch out Funding account Deposits landing, P2P buys and sells, and withdrawals often use this On most platforms, coins kept here cannot be used directly for a spot or contract order Spot account Spot trades and instant convert To withdraw, you may need to move funds back to the funding account first Contract account Margin for contract trading Open positions can limit transfers out. Losses are taken from this balance Transfer between accounts Moving assets between accounts on the same platform Internal bookkeeping. It does not go on-chain, usually has no network fee, and arrives almost at once On-chain withdrawal Sending coins from the exchange to an external wallet or another platform's address This really goes on-chain. You choose a network, enter an address, pay a withdrawal fee, and cannot undo it after it arrives If \"the balance disappeared\", check whether it is sitting in another account. If an order says \"insufficient balance\", you may need to move the asset into the account that order uses. Names and transfer rules differ by platform. Some platforms merge spot and contracts into a \"unified account\". Follow the app screen and the official explanation of the platform you use."}]}, {"id": "05", "title": "CEX exchange guide", "emoji": "🏦", "intro": "Official entrances, and how to choose", "tags": ["Guide"], "tip": "Note: Look at the domain before you click, the rules before you open an account, and platform risk before you deposit.", "sections": [{"title": "What can a CEX do for you?", "html": "<p><strong>Coins in an exchange account are held by the platform. That account is not your own wallet.</strong></p>\n<p>A CEX usually offers accounts, deposits, withdrawals, and trading. Some also offer fiat channels, derivatives, or other products. Whether it is open to you depends on region, identity, and the rules of the specific product.</p>\n<p>A balance in a platform account is usually the platform's internal record. You rely on the operator to hold the assets and to process withdrawals. Do not treat it as a wallet whose private key you control.</p>", "text": "Coins in an exchange account are held by the platform. That account is not your own wallet. A CEX usually offers accounts, deposits, withdrawals, and trading. Some also offer fiat channels, derivatives, or other products. Whether it is open to you depends on region, identity, and the rules of the specific product. A balance in a platform account is usually the platform's internal record. You rely on the operator to hold the assets and to process withdrawals. Do not treat it as a wallet whose private key you control."}, {"title": "How is a CEX different from a DEX?", "html": "<p><strong>A CEX is a company keeping the books for you. A DEX is you trading on-chain from your wallet.</strong></p>\n<p>A CEX is a centralized exchange. The operator usually manages accounts, orders, and the platform's internal asset records. You trade through the platform.</p>\n<p>A DEX is a decentralized trading protocol or venue. Many DEXs swap on-chain through smart contracts. You sign from your wallet. Matching and settlement can still work in different ways.</p>\n<p>A DEX still has centralized pieces. The front end, admins, oracles, and other components can add risk.</p>", "text": "A CEX is a company keeping the books for you. A DEX is you trading on-chain from your wallet. A CEX is a centralized exchange. The operator usually manages accounts, orders, and the platform's internal asset records. You trade through the platform. A DEX is a decentralized trading protocol or venue. Many DEXs swap on-chain through smart contracts. You sign from your wallet. Matching and settlement can still work in different ways. A DEX still has centralized pieces. The front end, admins, oracles, and other components can add risk."}, {"title": "Referral sign-up links", "html": "<p><strong>When you sign up, check whether the platform serves your region, then use the entrances below.</strong></p>\n<p>Only Binance, Bybit, Gate, and HTX are shown here. The order is not a safety ranking or a recommendation. Before you use one, confirm that registration and the related services are available where you are.</p><div data-widget=\"exchanges\"></div>", "text": "When you sign up, check whether the platform serves your region, then use the entrances below. Only Binance, Bybit, Gate, and HTX are shown here. The order is not a safety ranking or a recommendation. Before you use one, confirm that registration and the related services are available where you are. | Platform | Referral sign-up link | |---|---| | Binance | https://accounts.binance.info/zh-CN/register?ref=I1GN6RMJ | | Bybit | https://partner.bybit.com/b/0xmay | | Gate | https://www.gatewebsite.app/share/u1dgb10j | | HTX | https://www.htx.com/invite/zh-cn/1h?invite_code=jdz98223 | Card label: referral link. Card note: this is the platform's promotional sign-up domain. After you register, check the account in the official app."}, {"title": "On-chain trading tools", "html": "<p><strong>If you want to buy and sell tokens directly on-chain, these two tools are the ones shown here.</strong></p>\n<p>DeBot and Binance Web3 Wallet are both for buying and selling tokens that have already been issued (the secondary market). They are not a primary-market subscription. New on-chain coins are very risky. Read chapter 09 before you touch them.</p><div data-widget=\"onchain\"></div>", "text": "If you want to buy and sell tokens directly on-chain, these two tools are the ones shown here. DeBot and Binance Web3 Wallet are both for buying and selling tokens that have already been issued (the secondary market). They are not a primary-market subscription. New on-chain coins are very risky. Read chapter 09 before you touch them."}, {"title": "How do you choose?", "html": "<p><strong>First check that you can use it. Then look at safety, fees, and withdrawals. Do not choose only for a sign-up bonus.</strong></p>\n<p>Check whether your location can use it. Then look at what you need to trade, how you will deposit, depth in the markets you care about, fees, security settings, withdrawal rules, and support channels. Do not decide only because of a sign-up reward or because a KOL recommended it.</p>", "text": "First check that you can use it. Then look at safety, fees, and withdrawals. Do not choose only for a sign-up bonus. Check whether your location can use it. Then look at what you need to trade, how you will deposit, depth in the markets you care about, fees, security settings, withdrawal rules, and support channels. Do not decide only because of a sign-up reward or because a KOL recommended it."}, {"title": "What does proof of reserves mean?", "html": "<p><strong>Proof of reserves is a snapshot at one moment. It does not mean the platform is safe forever.</strong></p>\n<p>Proof of reserves can show assets or liabilities checked at a specific time and within a specific scope. How much that is worth depends on the method and what it covers. It is not automatically a full financial audit. It cannot guarantee that nothing will go wrong later, or that you can withdraw whenever you want.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>📰 <strong>A real case: the FTX collapse (2022)</strong><br />\nBefore its 2022 bankruptcy, FTX ranked third in the world by trading volume (according to a November report that year by The Verge). On 2 November 2022, problems in its balance sheet were reported. Six days later (8 November) it stopped all crypto withdrawals. In the first 72 hours, users had already withdrawn about 6 billion dollars. On 11 November it filed for bankruptcy protection. Founder SBF was later sentenced to 25 years.<br />\n<strong>The lesson: a large platform can still fail. Watch an exchange's news and announcements. If withdrawals slow down or bad news piles up, consider moving assets out while you still can. That is not always possible, and moving them is not a guarantee of safety.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n", "text": "Proof of reserves is a snapshot at one moment. It does not mean the platform is safe forever. Proof of reserves can show assets or liabilities checked at a specific time and within a specific scope. How much that is worth depends on the method and what it covers. It is not automatically a full financial audit. It cannot guarantee that nothing will go wrong later, or that you can withdraw whenever you want. 📰 A real case: the FTX collapse (2022) Before its 2022 bankruptcy, FTX ranked third in the world by trading volume (according to a November report that year by The Verge). On 2 November 2022, problems in its balance sheet were reported. Six days later (8 November) it stopped all crypto withdrawals. In the first 72 hours, users had already withdrawn about 6 billion dollars. On 11 November it filed for bankruptcy protection. Founder SBF was later sentenced to 25 years. The lesson: a large platform can still fail. Watch an exchange's news and announcements. If withdrawals slow down or bad news piles up, consider moving assets out while you still can. That is not always possible, and moving them is not a guarantee of safety. Note: Look at the domain before you click, the rules before you open an account, and platform risk before you deposit."}]}, {"id": "06", "title": "Spot orders for beginners", "emoji": "🧾", "intro": "Place an order, read a candle, and keep your nerve", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "What is spot?", "html": "<p><strong>Spot means you buy the coin outright. You are not borrowing, and you are not using leverage.</strong></p>\n<p>Spot is buying and selling the asset itself. An ordinary spot position with no borrowing is not liquidated just because the price falls. The asset's price can still go close to zero.</p>\n<p>For leverage and contracts, see <a href=\"#/c/07\">Chapter 07, &quot;Going further: leverage and risk&quot;</a>.</p>", "text": "Spot means you buy the coin outright. You are not borrowing, and you are not using leverage. Spot is buying and selling the asset itself. An ordinary spot position with no borrowing is not liquidated just because the price falls. The asset's price can still go close to zero. For leverage and contracts, see Chapter 07, \"Going further: leverage and risk\" ."}, {"title": "Order types", "html": "<p><strong>A market order fills quickly, at an uncertain price. A limit order sets the price, and may not fill.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Easy to misunderstand</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Market</td>\n<td>Fills as soon as it can, at the available quote</td>\n<td>It does not guarantee one fixed price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit</td>\n<td>Sets the highest buy price or the lowest sell price</td>\n<td>It may not fill, or it may fill only in part</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop-market</td>\n<td>After the trigger, it submits a market order</td>\n<td>In a violent move, slippage can be large</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop-limit</td>\n<td>After the trigger, it submits a limit order</td>\n<td>It can trigger and still fail to fill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Platforms differ in which orders they offer and which price triggers them. Read the description before you submit.</p>", "text": "A market order fills quickly, at an uncertain price. A limit order sets the price, and may not fill. Type What it does Easy to misunderstand Market Fills as soon as it can, at the available quote It does not guarantee one fixed price Limit Sets the highest buy price or the lowest sell price It may not fill, or it may fill only in part Stop-market After the trigger, it submits a market order In a violent move, slippage can be large Stop-limit After the trigger, it submits a limit order It can trigger and still fail to fill Platforms differ in which orders they offer and which price triggers them. Read the description before you submit."}, {"title": "Candle basics", "html": "<p><strong>One candle records four prices for a stretch of time: open, high, low, and close.</strong></p>\n<p>A candle usually records the open, high, low, and close of that interval. 1 minute, 15 minutes, 1 hour, and 1 day are different scales.</p>\n<p>The same asset can look like a different trend on a different timeframe. Up and down colors are a platform setting. Do not judge only by red and green.</p>", "text": "One candle records four prices for a stretch of time: open, high, low, and close. A candle usually records the open, high, low, and close of that interval. 1 minute, 15 minutes, 1 hour, and 1 day are different scales. The same asset can look like a different trend on a different timeframe. Up and down colors are a platform setting. Do not judge only by red and green."}, {"title": "A trade log", "html": "<p><strong>Write the reason before the order, and the review after. That beats staring at one more indicator.</strong></p>\n<p>Before the order, write: why you are buying, what would show the idea was wrong, where you plan to exit, the size, the fees, and the loss you might take.<br />\nAfter the trade, write: whether you followed the plan, where it drifted, and whether the result came from the plan or from luck.</p>\n<p>Practice with a paper trade first. Paper profits are not real-trading results. Real money brings emotion, depth, and execution cost.</p>", "text": "Write the reason before the order, and the review after. That beats staring at one more indicator. Before the order, write: why you are buying, what would show the idea was wrong, where you plan to exit, the size, the fees, and the loss you might take. After the trade, write: whether you followed the plan, where it drifted, and whether the result came from the plan or from luck. Practice with a paper trade first. Paper profits are not real-trading results. Real money brings emotion, depth, and execution cost."}, {"title": "Three moments when people lose their head", "html": "<p><strong>Fear of missing out, rushing to get back to even, and a winning streak are when impulses show up.</strong></p>\n<p>Fear of missing out: other people are making money, and you chase.<br />\nRushing to get back to even: after a loss, you size up or add leverage.<br />\nA winning streak: you treat a short run of luck as a stable skill.</p>\n<p>When you notice one of these, pause and reread the plan. That helps more than hunting for another indicator.</p>", "text": "Fear of missing out, rushing to get back to even, and a winning streak are when impulses show up. Fear of missing out: other people are making money, and you chase. Rushing to get back to even: after a loss, you size up or add leverage. A winning streak: you treat a short run of luck as a stable skill. When you notice one of these, pause and reread the plan. That helps more than hunting for another indicator."}]}, {"id": "07", "title": "Going further: leverage and risk", "emoji": "⚖️", "intro": "Leverage, contracts, liquidation, and position size", "tags": ["Advanced"], "sections": [{"title": "Leverage and contracts", "html": "<p><strong>Leverage magnifies both gains and losses. Beginners should leave it alone.</strong></p>\n<p>Leveraged trading uses borrowing or margin to make the exposure larger. Gains and losses both get bigger. There is also a borrowing cost and liquidation risk.</p>\n<p>Contract trading is a bet on a derivative exposure. You do not necessarily hold the underlying spot asset. A perpetual contract has no traditional fixed expiry. Funding rates and similar mechanisms are commonly used to keep its price near a reference price.</p>", "text": "Leverage magnifies both gains and losses. Beginners should leave it alone. Leveraged trading uses borrowing or margin to make the exposure larger. Gains and losses both get bigger. There is also a borrowing cost and liquidation risk. Contract trading is a bet on a derivative exposure. You do not necessarily hold the underlying spot asset. A perpetual contract has no traditional fixed expiry. Funding rates and similar mechanisms are commonly used to keep its price near a reference price."}, {"title": "Long and short", "html": "<p><strong>Long bets on a rise. Short bets on a fall. A short often means borrowing the coin or using a contract.</strong></p>\n<p>A long position hopes to profit if the price rises. A short hopes to profit if it falls. Shorting is not as simple as selling spot you already hold. It often involves borrowing or a derivative.</p>", "text": "Long bets on a rise. Short bets on a fall. A short often means borrowing the coin or using a contract. A long position hopes to profit if the price rises. A short hopes to profit if it falls. Shorting is not as simple as selling spot you already hold. It often involves borrowing or a derivative."}, {"title": "Margin, cross, and isolated", "html": "<p><strong>Cross margin can drag in the rest of the account. Isolated margin loses only the margin on that position.</strong></p>\n<p>Margin is the funds backing a position. Isolated margin limits that backing to one position. Cross margin lets more of the account's available funds support the position. The exact rules depend on the platform and the account mode.</p>\n<p>Cross margin can put more of the account at risk. Isolated margin is not risk-free either.</p>", "text": "Cross margin can drag in the rest of the account. Isolated margin loses only the margin on that position. Margin is the funds backing a position. Isolated margin limits that backing to one position. Cross margin lets more of the account's available funds support the position. The exact rules depend on the platform and the account mode. Cross margin can put more of the account at risk. Isolated margin is not risk-free either."}, {"title": "Liquidation", "html": "<p><strong>When margin is not enough, the platform closes the position for you. That is liquidation.</strong></p>\n<p>When margin or other conditions fall below what the platform requires, the system may shrink or close the position. The trigger often uses a mark price and other rules, not the last traded price you are looking at.</p>\n<p>Do not treat \"10x leverage means it liquidates only after a 10% drop\" as an exact formula. Maintenance margin, fees, position size, and the platform's rules all change the result.</p>", "text": "When margin is not enough, the platform closes the position for you. That is liquidation. When margin or other conditions fall below what the platform requires, the system may shrink or close the position. The trigger often uses a mark price and other rules, not the last traded price you are looking at. Do not treat \"10x leverage means it liquidates only after a 10% drop\" as an exact formula. Maintenance margin, fees, position size, and the platform's rules all change the result."}, {"title": "Funding rate", "html": "<p><strong>Hold a perpetual for a long time, and funding can keep eating the profit (sometimes you are the one getting paid).</strong></p>\n<p>A perpetual periodically settles a funding payment between long and short holders. Direction, interval, and rules differ by platform. On a position you hold for a long time, that fee changes the result in a real way.</p>", "text": "Hold a perpetual for a long time, and funding can keep eating the profit (sometimes you are the one getting paid). A perpetual periodically settles a funding payment between long and short holders. Direction, interval, and rules differ by platform. On a position you hold for a long time, that fee changes the result in a real way."}, {"title": "Position size and a risk budget", "html": "<p><strong>Decide the most this idea is allowed to lose. Then work out how much to buy.</strong></p>\n<p>First decide \"if this idea is wrong, how much am I prepared to lose?\" Then work out the size. That risk amount is not the money you put in, and it is not the margin.</p>\n<p><strong>A learning example of a risk rule: plan to risk 1% of account funds on one idea. If you want to be more aggressive, treat 2% as the ceiling.</strong> This is not a safety standard that fits everyone. If you are unsure, use a lower share, or do not trade.</p>\n<p>Suppose the account has <strong>10,000 USDT (often written 10,000 U)</strong>, and the planned risk on this idea is 1%. Three steps:</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: how much are you allowed to lose?</strong><br />\n10,000 × 1% = <strong>100 USDT</strong>. If the stop is hit as planned, you want the loss on this idea kept near 100 USDT.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: if you buy a whole BTC, how much do you lose at the stop?</strong><br />\nPlan to buy at 60,000 USDT and stop at 59,000 USDT.<br />\nThe gap per BTC is 60,000 − 59,000 = <strong>1,000 USDT</strong>.<br />\nSo 1 BTC, bought and sold at those two prices, loses 1,000 USDT. 0.1 BTC loses 100 USDT.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: so how much do you buy?</strong><br />\nYou can lose 100 USDT. Each coin might lose 1,000 USDT.<br />\n100 ÷ 1,000 = <strong>0.1 BTC</strong>.<br />\nAt entry, the notional value of that position is 0.1 × 60,000 = <strong>6,000 USDT</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula: position size = (account funds × planned risk share) ÷ |entry price − stop price|.</strong><br />\nThe result is a quantity of the asset, for example a number of BTC. Multiply by the entry price to get the notional value. The absolute value keeps the gap positive for both a buy and a short. This fits a simplified spot trade or a linear contract. An inverse contract has to be calculated under its own rules.</p>\n<p><strong>What does leverage change here?</strong><br />\nThe same 0.1 BTC position, simplified: about 3,000 USDT of initial margin at 2x, and about 600 USDT at 10x. The quantity and the buy and sell prices are the same, so the profit or loss from the price move is still the same. Using only 600 USDT of margin does not make this loss smaller.</p>\n<p>But higher leverage pulls liquidation closer. The planned risk above holds only if the position stays the same, you are not liquidated early, and the stop fills as expected. Fees, funding, slippage, and a failed execution also change the real loss. Leave room in the budget for costs, and check where liquidation sits. Do not treat \"100 U\" as a guaranteed ceiling you cannot go past.</p>\n<p>Diversifying still depends on correlation. Holding several coins that move together may not actually spread the risk.</p>", "text": "Decide the most this idea is allowed to lose. Then work out how much to buy. First decide \"if this idea is wrong, how much am I prepared to lose?\" Then work out the size. That risk amount is not the money you put in, and it is not the margin. A learning example of a risk rule: plan to risk 1% of account funds on one idea. If you want to be more aggressive, treat 2% as the ceiling. This is not a safety standard that fits everyone. If you are unsure, use a lower share, or do not trade. Suppose the account has 10,000 USDT (often written 10,000 U) , and the planned risk on this idea is 1%. Three steps: Step 1: how much are you allowed to lose? 10,000 × 1% = 100 USDT . If the stop is hit as planned, you want the loss on this idea kept near 100 USDT. Step 2: if you buy a whole BTC, how much do you lose at the stop? Plan to buy at 60,000 USDT and stop at 59,000 USDT. The gap per BTC is 60,000 − 59,000 = 1,000 USDT . So 1 BTC, bought and sold at those two prices, loses 1,000 USDT. 0.1 BTC loses 100 USDT. Step 3: so how much do you buy? You can lose 100 USDT. Each coin might lose 1,000 USDT. 100 ÷ 1,000 = 0.1 BTC . At entry, the notional value of that position is 0.1 × 60,000 = 6,000 USDT . Formula: position size = (account funds × planned risk share) ÷ |entry price − stop price|. The result is a quantity of the asset, for example a number of BTC. Multiply by the entry price to get the notional value. The absolute value keeps the gap positive for both a buy and a short. This fits a simplified spot trade or a linear contract. An inverse contract has to be calculated under its own rules. What does leverage change here? The same 0.1 BTC position, simplified: about 3,000 USDT of initial margin at 2x, and about 600 USDT at 10x. The quantity and the buy and sell prices are the same, so the profit or loss from the price move is still the same. Using only 600 USDT of margin does not make this loss smaller. But higher leverage pulls liquidation closer. The planned risk above holds only if the position stays the same, you are not liquidated early, and the stop fills as expected. Fees, funding, slippage, and a failed execution also change the real loss. Leave room in the budget for costs, and check where liquidation sits. Do not treat \"100 U\" as a guaranteed ceiling you cannot go past. Diversifying still depends on correlation. Holding several coins that move together may not actually spread the risk."}, {"title": "Indicators", "html": "<p><strong>Indicators only describe past data. They do not predict tomorrow.</strong></p>\n<p>Volume helps you see how much participation there was. Support and resistance are areas where buying or selling has shown up, or might. They are not a wall that guarantees a turn.</p>\n<p>One breakout, one big green candle, or one bounce cannot by itself confirm what comes next.</p>\n<p>EMA: exponential moving average. A weighted way to watch the price trend.<br />\nRSI: relative strength index. It looks at the relative strength of price changes over a period.<br />\nMACD: watches trend and momentum through moving-average relationships and related calculations.<br />\nStoch RSI: a stochastic calculation applied to RSI. It is usually more sensitive.</p>\n<p>Bollinger Bands: often a middle band plus upper and lower bands, used to see where price sits and how volatility is changing. A narrow band means recent volatility shrank. It does not by itself say the next move is up or down. Touching the upper band does not mean the price is about to fall.</p>\n<p>Fibonacci retracement / extension: pick the high and low of a move, then mark possible pullback zones or extension targets by ratio. Common retracement ratios include 38.2% and 61.8%. Tools often also show 50%. A different start point gives a different result. The line is not a price the market must reach.</p>\n<p>Divergence: price and an indicator do not make a new high or a new low together. For example, price makes a new high and RSI does not, which suggests upside momentum is weakening. Price makes a new low and RSI does not, which suggests downside momentum is weakening. Divergence is an observation, not a standalone indicator, and it does not guarantee an immediate reversal.</p>\n<p>An indicator is a processing of historical data. A high RSI does not mean the price is about to fall. A golden cross is not a sure profit. Being able to say when it fails, in which conditions, is more useful than memorizing a slogan.</p>", "text": "Indicators only describe past data. They do not predict tomorrow. Volume helps you see how much participation there was. Support and resistance are areas where buying or selling has shown up, or might. They are not a wall that guarantees a turn. One breakout, one big green candle, or one bounce cannot by itself confirm what comes next. EMA: exponential moving average. A weighted way to watch the price trend. RSI: relative strength index. It looks at the relative strength of price changes over a period. MACD: watches trend and momentum through moving-average relationships and related calculations. Stoch RSI: a stochastic calculation applied to RSI. It is usually more sensitive. Bollinger Bands: often a middle band plus upper and lower bands, used to see where price sits and how volatility is changing. A narrow band means recent volatility shrank. It does not by itself say the next move is up or down. Touching the upper band does not mean the price is about to fall. Fibonacci retracement / extension: pick the high and low of a move, then mark possible pullback zones or extension targets by ratio. Common retracement ratios include 38.2% and 61.8%. Tools often also show 50%. A different start point gives a different result. The line is not a price the market must reach. Divergence: price and an indicator do not make a new high or a new low together. For example, price makes a new high and RSI does not, which suggests upside momentum is weakening. Price makes a new low and RSI does not, which suggests downside momentum is weakening. Divergence is an observation, not a standalone indicator, and it does not guarantee an immediate reversal. An indicator is a processing of historical data. A high RSI does not mean the price is about to fall. A golden cross is not a sure profit. Being able to say when it fails, in which conditions, is more useful than memorizing a slogan."}]}, {"id": "08", "title": "Wallets, cold wallets, and CEX", "emoji": "👛", "intro": "Where the coins sit, and who holds the keys", "tags": ["Basics"], "sections": [{"title": "What is a wallet?", "html": "<p><strong>A wallet does not hold the coins. It holds the keys that can move them.</strong></p>\n<p>A wallet helps you manage an account, see assets, and sign actions. The asset record lives on the relevant chain. What the wallet usually manages is the credential that accesses and signs for those assets, not a pile of coins stored inside the phone.</p>\n<p>Different wallets may use a seed phrase, a smart account, multi-party computation, or another recovery method. You need to understand the specific scheme you are using.</p>", "text": "A wallet does not hold the coins. It holds the keys that can move them. A wallet helps you manage an account, see assets, and sign actions. The asset record lives on the relevant chain. What the wallet usually manages is the credential that accesses and signs for those assets, not a pile of coins stored inside the phone. Different wallets may use a seed phrase, a smart account, multi-party computation, or another recovery method. You need to understand the specific scheme you are using."}, {"title": "Address, private key, seed phrase", "html": "<p><strong>You can give someone an address. You cannot give anyone a private key or a seed phrase.</strong></p>\n<p>Address: used to receive funds or to identify an on-chain account. You can share it, but that creates a privacy link.<br />\nPrivate key: the secret that authorizes actions on the account. Do not share it.<br />\nSeed phrase: on some wallets, the word list that generates and restores a set of keys. If it leaks, it can affect several accounts under that phrase.</p>\n<p>A wallet password only protects the local app or file. It is not control of the chain. Changing the app password does not invalidate a seed phrase that has already leaked.</p>", "text": "You can give someone an address. You cannot give anyone a private key or a seed phrase. Address: used to receive funds or to identify an on-chain account. You can share it, but that creates a privacy link. Private key: the secret that authorizes actions on the account. Do not share it. Seed phrase: on some wallets, the word list that generates and restores a set of keys. If it leaks, it can affect several accounts under that phrase. A wallet password only protects the local app or file. It is not control of the chain. Changing the app password does not invalidate a seed phrase that has already leaked."}, {"title": "Two different ways to classify wallets", "html": "<p><strong>Custody is about who holds the keys. Hot and cold are about whether those keys are online.</strong></p>\n<p>Custodial / self-custody: the question is who controls the keys and the actions on the assets.<br />\nHot / cold wallet: the question is whether the keys are in an environment that is online.</p>\n<p>A hardware wallet is a common cold-storage and signing tool. Do not treat \"any hardware device\" as automatically a trustworthy cold wallet.</p>", "text": "Custody is about who holds the keys. Hot and cold are about whether those keys are online. Custodial / self-custody: the question is who controls the keys and the actions on the assets. Hot / cold wallet: the question is whether the keys are in an environment that is online. A hardware wallet is a common cold-storage and signing tool. Do not treat \"any hardware device\" as automatically a trustworthy cold wallet."}, {"title": "CEX, software wallet, hardware wallet", "html": "<p><strong>A platform account is convenient, and you depend on the platform. Self-custody is freer, and the responsibility is yours.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>CEX account</th>\n<th>Self-custody software wallet</th>\n<th>Self-custody hardware wallet</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who is in control</td>\n<td>Usually the platform holds the assets</td>\n<td>You manage the keys under that wallet's scheme</td>\n<td>You manage them; the device helps you sign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day to day</td>\n<td>Trading inside the platform is relatively convenient</td>\n<td>On-chain use is relatively convenient</td>\n<td>Signing has an extra check on the device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How you recover</td>\n<td>The platform's account recovery and review</td>\n<td>A seed phrase, or that wallet's own recovery</td>\n<td>A backup, plus recovery that is specific to the device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main risks</td>\n<td>The platform, the account, withdrawal limits, and similar</td>\n<td>Malware, phishing, the backup, and signing the wrong thing</td>\n<td>Supply chain, the backup, a bad signature, and using the device wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What you are responsible for</td>\n<td>Account security and choosing the platform</td>\n<td>Keys, backup, and safe interaction</td>\n<td>Keys, backup, the device, and safe interaction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>", "text": "A platform account is convenient, and you depend on the platform. Self-custody is freer, and the responsibility is yours. Item CEX account Self-custody software wallet Self-custody hardware wallet Who is in control Usually the platform holds the assets You manage the keys under that wallet's scheme You manage them; the device helps you sign Day to day Trading inside the platform is relatively convenient On-chain use is relatively convenient Signing has an extra check on the device How you recover The platform's account recovery and review A seed phrase, or that wallet's own recovery A backup, plus recovery that is specific to the device Main risks The platform, the account, withdrawal limits, and similar Malware, phishing, the backup, and signing the wrong thing Supply chain, the backup, a bad signature, and using the device wrong What you are responsible for Account security and choosing the platform Keys, backup, and safe interaction Keys, backup, the device, and safe interaction"}, {"title": "A keyless wallet beginners can start with 🧸", "html": "<p><strong>Don't want to write down a seed phrase? A keyless wallet splits the key into three shares. That can be easier for a beginner.</strong></p>\n<p>Worried you will mishandle a private key, or lose a seed phrase? Look at Binance Wallet (keyless).</p>\n<p>It uses MPC (multi-party computation). The key is split into 3 shares: one on your device, one in a personal cloud backup, and one held by Binance. Any two shares together can use the wallet. Two of them are in your hands, so <strong>Binance alone cannot move your money</strong>. You do not write down a traditional seed phrase when you create it.</p>\n<p>Creating Binance Wallet (keyless) requires a Binance account that has already passed identity checks. It can also import an existing seed phrase or private-key wallet, and you can convert the keyless wallet into a private-key wallet and hold that yourself.</p>\n<h3>How is it different from a Binance exchange account?</h3>\n<p>The exchange account is for buying, selling, and managing funds inside the platform. Binance Wallet (keyless) is for holding on-chain assets, transferring them, and using Web3 apps. You can reach both from the Binance app. They are different ways of holding assets.</p>\n<h3>How do you start?</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the official Binance app and go to Wallet.</li>\n<li>Choose Create wallet, and follow the prompts to create a keyless wallet.</li>\n<li>Finish the backup. Binance officially offers quick backup, cloud backup, and QR backup. Finish the one you chose as the app instructs. Confirm which accounts, passwords, or materials recovery needs. Then deposit assets.</li>\n<li>Practice receiving, sending, and on-chain actions with a small amount first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: an encrypted backup in the wallet's official flow is not the same as screenshotting a seed phrase and uploading it to a cloud drive. Still do not photograph, screenshot, or upload a seed phrase or a private key.</p>\n<p>Sources: <a class=\"src\" href=\"https://www.binance.com/en/support/faq/detail/048ee79532494c03918dc4004214ad11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.binance.com/en/support/faq/detail/048ee79532494c03918dc4004214ad11\">Binance FAQ: What is Binance Wallet ↗</a>, <a class=\"src\" href=\"https://www.binance.com/en/blog/ecosystem/7898832595548730429\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.binance.com/en/blog/ecosystem/7898832595548730429\">Binance blog: self-custody Web3 wallet ↗</a></p>\n<div data-widget=\"binancewallet\"></div>", "text": "Don't want to write down a seed phrase? A keyless wallet splits the key into three shares. That can be easier for a beginner. Worried you will mishandle a private key, or lose a seed phrase? Look at Binance Wallet (keyless). It uses MPC (multi-party computation). The key is split into 3 shares: one on your device, one in a personal cloud backup, and one held by Binance. Any two shares together can use the wallet. Two of them are in your hands, so Binance alone cannot move your money. You do not write down a traditional seed phrase when you create it. Creating Binance Wallet (keyless) requires a Binance account that has already passed identity checks. It can also import an existing seed phrase or private-key wallet, and you can convert the keyless wallet into a private-key wallet and hold that yourself. ### How is it different from a Binance exchange account? The exchange account is for buying, selling, and managing funds inside the platform. Binance Wallet (keyless) is for holding on-chain assets, transferring them, and using Web3 apps. You can reach both from the Binance app. They are different ways of holding assets. ### How do you start? 1. Open the official Binance app and go to Wallet. 2. Choose Create wallet, and follow the prompts to create a keyless wallet. 3. Finish the backup. Binance officially offers quick backup, cloud backup, and QR backup. Finish the one you chose as the app instructs. Confirm which accounts, passwords, or materials recovery needs. Then deposit assets. 4. Practice receiving, sending, and on-chain actions with a small amount first. Note: an encrypted backup in the wallet's official flow is not the same as screenshotting a seed phrase and uploading it to a cloud drive. Still do not photograph, screenshot, or upload a seed phrase or a private key. Sources: [Binance FAQ: What is Binance Wallet](https://www.binance.com/en/support/faq/detail/048ee79532494c03918dc4004214ad11), [Binance blog: self-custody Web3 wallet](https://www.binance.com/en/blog/ecosystem/7898832595548730429) Buttons: binance-wallet"}, {"title": "Do you need a hardware wallet?", "html": "<p><strong>You do not have to buy a hardware wallet on day one. Learn to back up, and learn to read what you are signing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You do not need to buy one the moment you arrive. Protecting the account, backing up correctly, and understanding signatures matter more at the start.</strong></p>\n<p>If you are only learning with a small amount, and you trade fairly often, you may not need a hardware wallet immediately. You can start with a platform account or a software wallet that fits the job, and understand the custody and device risks of each.</p>\n<p>Whether you need one should not be decided by a single line like \"only if you are worth hundreds of millions\". If an amount already matters a lot to you, and you plan to self-custody it for a long time, it is worth studying a hardware wallet even when the amount is far below that.</p>\n<p>A hardware wallet can reduce some of the risk of keys touching an online environment. It is not a magic safe. A leaked backup, a swapped device, or you actively signing a malicious action can still lose the assets.</p>\n<p><strong>What decides it is how much that sum matters to you, how long you will hold it, and whether you can operate the setup. Not one universal money threshold.</strong></p>", "text": "You do not have to buy a hardware wallet on day one. Learn to back up, and learn to read what you are signing. You do not need to buy one the moment you arrive. Protecting the account, backing up correctly, and understanding signatures matter more at the start. If you are only learning with a small amount, and you trade fairly often, you may not need a hardware wallet immediately. You can start with a platform account or a software wallet that fits the job, and understand the custody and device risks of each. Whether you need one should not be decided by a single line like \"only if you are worth hundreds of millions\". If an amount already matters a lot to you, and you plan to self-custody it for a long time, it is worth studying a hardware wallet even when the amount is far below that. A hardware wallet can reduce some of the risk of keys touching an online environment. It is not a magic safe. A leaked backup, a swapped device, or you actively signing a malicious action can still lose the assets. What decides it is how much that sum matters to you, how long you will hold it, and whether you can operate the setup. Not one universal money threshold."}, {"title": "How do you separate funds?", "html": "<p><strong>Practice, trading, and long-term storage belong in different wallets. Do not keep them together.</strong></p>\n<p>Learning / interaction wallet: a small amount you can afford to lose, for trying apps.<br />\nEveryday trading funds: kept where you trade, with the custody risk understood.<br />\nLong-term savings wallet: fewer interactions, and a storage setup that fits you.</p>\n<p>Creating several accounts under the same seed phrase does not isolate you from a leaked phrase. To separate that risk, you need an independent key or recovery scheme.</p>", "text": "Practice, trading, and long-term storage belong in different wallets. Do not keep them together. Learning / interaction wallet: a small amount you can afford to lose, for trying apps. Everyday trading funds: kept where you trade, with the custody risk understood. Long-term savings wallet: fewer interactions, and a storage setup that fits you. Creating several accounts under the same seed phrase does not isolate you from a leaked phrase. To separate that risk, you need an independent key or recovery scheme."}, {"title": "What if the device is lost?", "html": "<p><strong>A lost phone is not the scary part. Having no backup is.</strong></p>\n<p>Losing the device does not automatically mean the assets are gone. Whether you can recover depends on the backup and the wallet's scheme. <strong>So finish the backup when you create the wallet, and confirm by the official method that the backup can actually restore it. Do not wait until the phone breaks or the device is gone.</strong> For a seed-phrase wallet, the words, the order, and the related recovery details all have to be exact. Restore in a trusted environment, following the official instructions. If you suspect someone else has the device or the backup, consider moving what remains to an account that uses a brand-new key.</p>", "text": "A lost phone is not the scary part. Having no backup is. Losing the device does not automatically mean the assets are gone. Whether you can recover depends on the backup and the wallet's scheme. So finish the backup when you create the wallet, and confirm by the official method that the backup can actually restore it. Do not wait until the phone breaks or the device is gone. For a seed-phrase wallet, the words, the order, and the related recovery details all have to be exact. Restore in a trusted environment, following the official instructions. If you suspect someone else has the device or the backup, consider moving what remains to an account that uses a brand-new key."}]}, {"id": "09", "title": "Safety and scam guide", "emoji": "🛡️", "intro": "Protect your account, seed phrase, and approvals", "tags": ["Must-read"], "tip": "Note: Safety is not just one cold wallet. It is a set of habits you keep.", "sections": [{"title": "Seed phrase and private key: do not share, do not upload", "html": "<p><strong>Do not give a seed phrase or a private key to anyone, and real support will not ask for it either.</strong></p>\n<p>Do not send it to support, to a friend, to a project, or to an AI.<br />\nDo not type it into any web form.<br />\nDo not photograph it and upload it to a cloud drive, and do not paste it into a chat tool or an online note.</p>\n<p>Back it up with the offline method the wallet officially supports.<br />\nAlso think about fire, water, loss, and the chance that someone else could reach that backup.</p>\n<p>Real support does not need your seed phrase to look up a transaction.<br />\nIf anyone asks for it, stop, and do not keep talking.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Real support does not need a seed phrase. If someone asks, stop the conversation.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not give a seed phrase to support, and do not photograph it and upload it first. If someone asks, stop.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone claiming to be support says they must have your seed phrase before they can look up your transaction. Should you give it?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, or they cannot look it up</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Real support does not need a seed phrase</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Photograph it, upload it to a cloud drive, then send it</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Do not give a seed phrase or a private key to anyone, and real support will not ask for it either. Do not send it to support, to a friend, to a project, or to an AI. Do not type it into any web form. Do not photograph it and upload it to a cloud drive, and do not paste it into a chat tool or an online note. Back it up with the offline method the wallet officially supports. Also think about fire, water, loss, and the chance that someone else could reach that backup. Real support does not need your seed phrase to look up a transaction. If anyone asks for it, stop, and do not keep talking. Think it through Someone claiming to be support says they must have your seed phrase before they can look up your transaction. Should you give it? A. Yes, or they cannot look it up B. No. Real support does not need a seed phrase C. Photograph it, upload it to a cloud drive, then send it"}, {"title": "Fake sites and fake apps", "html": "<p><strong>Links in search results and ads can be fake, so use only an official entrance you have already checked.</strong></p>\n<p>Search results, ads, social accounts, and pinned group messages can all carry lookalike links.<br />\nRead the full domain first, then cross-check it with a trusted official channel.<br />\nOnce an entrance is confirmed, save it and use that one later.</p>\n<p>When you download an app, check the developer and the source.<br />\nDo not install a package a stranger sends you in private.<br />\nBuy a hardware wallet from the official channel, or from a channel you can verify.<br />\nDo not use a seed phrase that someone else set up in advance.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. An ad rank or a search rank does not prove it is official. Check the domain and the developer, and use an entrance you have already verified.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Ranking first, or a package sent in private, does not make it official. Check the full domain and the developer first.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>An \"official download\" sits at the top of a search ad. Can you install it directly?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. First place means it is official</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Check the full domain and the developer, and use only an entrance you have already verified</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. An installer a stranger sends you is safer</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Links in search results and ads can be fake, so use only an official entrance you have already checked. Search results, ads, social accounts, and pinned group messages can all carry lookalike links. Read the full domain first, then cross-check it with a trusted official channel. Once an entrance is confirmed, save it and use that one later. When you download an app, check the developer and the source. Do not install a package a stranger sends you in private. Buy a hardware wallet from the official channel, or from a channel you can verify. Do not use a seed phrase that someone else set up in advance. Think it through An \"official download\" sits at the top of a search ad. Can you install it directly? A. Yes. First place means it is official B. No. Check the full domain and the developer, and use only an entrance you have already verified C. An installer a stranger sends you is safer"}, {"title": "Connecting, signing, and approving are not the same thing", "html": "<p><strong>Connecting a wallet does not usually move the coins, but a careless signature or a careless approval can.</strong></p>\n<p>Connecting a wallet usually lets an app see the address and request a permission.<br />\nThat does not by itself mean the assets have already been moved.</p>\n<p>A signature confirms a piece of information or one action.<br />\nSome off-chain signatures can also authorize a later transfer of assets.<br />\nDo not treat \"no gas fee\" as \"no risk\".</p>\n<p>An approval lets a contract use an asset under conditions, for example an ERC-20 token allowance.<br />\nDifferent networks and different assets use different permission mechanisms.</p>\n<p>If you cannot read what the request is asking, do not confirm it.<br />\nDo not sign blindly.<br />\nA page titled \"log in\" does not mean it is only a login.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>📰 <strong>A reported case: one signature, and about 1.39 million dollars was gone (October 2024)</strong></p>\n<p>A PEPE holder signed an off-chain Uniswap Permit2 signature on a phishing site (it cost no gas).<br />\nAbout an hour later, PEPE and other tokens in the wallet, worth about 1.39 million dollars, were transferred out.<br />\nAround October 10 of the same month, someone else lost 15,079 fwDETH to a Permit phishing signature (then worth about 35 to 36 million dollars).</p>\n<p><strong>The lesson: a signature that costs no gas can still move the coins. If you cannot read it, do not sign.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. No gas fee does not mean no risk. If you cannot read the request, do not confirm it.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. No gas fee, or a title that says log in, does not prove it is safe. If you cannot read it, do not sign.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>A signature costs no gas. Can you treat it as safe?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. If it costs no gas, it cannot touch the coins</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Some off-chain signatures can still authorize assets to be moved</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the page title says log in, it is definitely only a login</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Connecting a wallet does not usually move the coins, but a careless signature or a careless approval can. Connecting a wallet usually lets an app see the address and request a permission. That does not by itself mean the assets have already been moved. A signature confirms a piece of information or one action. Some off-chain signatures can also authorize a later transfer of assets. Do not treat \"no gas fee\" as \"no risk\". An approval lets a contract use an asset under conditions, for example an ERC-20 token allowance. Different networks and different assets use different permission mechanisms. If you cannot read what the request is asking, do not confirm it. Do not sign blindly. A page titled \"log in\" does not mean it is only a login. 📰 A reported case: one signature, and about 1.39 million dollars was gone (October 2024) A PEPE holder signed an off-chain Uniswap Permit2 signature on a phishing site (it cost no gas). About an hour later, PEPE and other tokens in the wallet, worth about 1.39 million dollars, were transferred out. Around October 10 of the same month, someone else lost 15,079 fwDETH to a Permit phishing signature (then worth about 35 to 36 million dollars). The lesson: a signature that costs no gas can still move the coins. If you cannot read it, do not sign. Think it through A signature costs no gas. Can you treat it as safe? A. Yes. If it costs no gas, it cannot touch the coins B. No. Some off-chain signatures can still authorize assets to be moved C. If the page title says log in, it is definitely only a login"}, {"title": "What can help you inspect a signature?", "html": "<p><strong>A security extension can warn you, and you still have to understand the request yourself.</strong></p>\n<p>GoPlus can help check tokens, approvals, and some on-chain risks.<br />\nThe result is only a reference. It cannot guarantee that assets are absolutely safe.<br />\nIt also offers a browser extension that, in the situations it supports, flags suspicious requests.<br />\nThe wallet's own transaction preview and risk warnings are worth reading too.</p>\n<p><strong>GoPlus official entrance:</strong> <a class=\"ext\" href=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">https://gopluslabs.io/en</a><span class=\"ext-meta\"><span class=\"domain\">🌐 gopluslabs.io</span><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></span></p>\n<p>From the official site, open the browser-extension page.<br />\nCheck the publisher, the wallets it works with, the networks, the permissions, and what it currently supports.<br />\nA signature-decoding API and a browser extension are not the same feature.<br />\nDo not assume the extension supports every chain and every kind of signature.</p>\n<p>A tool's warning is only a helper.<br />\nNo warning does not mean it is definitely safe.<br />\nYou still have to check the counterparty, the change in assets, and the scope of the approval.<br />\nNo check requires you to give the tool your seed phrase.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. No warning is only a helper. You still have to check the counterparty, the change in assets, and the scope of the approval yourself.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. No warning does not mean it is safe. Do not give a seed phrase to a checking tool.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>The checking extension showed no warning. Is this signature definitely safe?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. No warning means you can sign</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The tool is only a helper, and no warning does not mean it is definitely safe</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Give the extension your seed phrase, and then it can check thoroughly</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A security extension can warn you, and you still have to understand the request yourself. GoPlus can help check tokens, approvals, and some on-chain risks. The result is only a reference. It cannot guarantee that assets are absolutely safe. It also offers a browser extension that, in the situations it supports, flags suspicious requests. The wallet's own transaction preview and risk warnings are worth reading too. GoPlus official entrance: https://gopluslabs.io/en 🌐 gopluslabs.io ⏳ Not checked yet From the official site, open the browser-extension page. Check the publisher, the wallets it works with, the networks, the permissions, and what it currently supports. A signature-decoding API and a browser extension are not the same feature. Do not assume the extension supports every chain and every kind of signature. A tool's warning is only a helper. No warning does not mean it is definitely safe. You still have to check the counterparty, the change in assets, and the scope of the approval. No check requires you to give the tool your seed phrase. Think it through The checking extension showed no warning. Is this signature definitely safe? A. Yes. No warning means you can sign B. No. The tool is only a helper, and no warning does not mean it is definitely safe C. Give the extension your seed phrase, and then it can check thoroughly"}, {"title": "Disconnecting is not the same as revoking", "html": "<p><strong>After you disconnect a site, an approval you already gave is still there, and it has to be revoked separately.</strong></p>\n<p>Disconnecting only changes the current connection.<br />\nAn approval already written on-chain is still valid.<br />\nCheck it and revoke it on the matching chain, in the matching wallet.</p>\n<p>A revoke may cost a network fee.<br />\nIt also cannot guarantee that assets already moved will come back.<br />\nIf the seed phrase or private key has already leaked, revoking an approval is not enough.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Disconnecting does not cancel an on-chain approval. The approval has to be revoked on its own. If the private key has leaked, revoking alone is not enough.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Disconnecting is not the same as revoking an approval. After a private key leaks, revoking an approval does not solve it.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>After you disconnect a site in the wallet, is the old approval still there?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. No. Disconnecting is the same as revoking</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Yes. An approval has to be revoked separately, on the matching chain and in the matching wallet</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the private key has already leaked, revoking the approval is enough</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "After you disconnect a site, an approval you already gave is still there, and it has to be revoked separately. Disconnecting only changes the current connection. An approval already written on-chain is still valid. Check it and revoke it on the matching chain, in the matching wallet. A revoke may cost a network fee. It also cannot guarantee that assets already moved will come back. If the seed phrase or private key has already leaked, revoking an approval is not enough. Think it through After you disconnect a site in the wallet, is the old approval still there? A. No. Disconnecting is the same as revoking B. Yes. An approval has to be revoked separately, on the matching chain and in the matching wallet C. If the private key has already leaked, revoking the approval is enough"}, {"title": "Unlimited approval", "html": "<p><strong>An unlimited approval saves one confirmation, and if something goes wrong, a large amount of that coin can be moved.</strong></p>\n<p>An unlimited approval lets the other side use a very large token allowance, without a new approval each time.<br />\nIt is more convenient, and it also magnifies the loss from a contract bug or a malicious permission.<br />\nRead the scope of the permission first, then approve only the amount you need.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The convenience costs you a very large allowance. The other side, or a contract with a bug, may use far more than this one trade.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. An unlimited approval is not risk-free, and it does not revoke itself. It lets the other side use a very large allowance.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is the main difference between an unlimited approval and approving a specific amount each time?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It only saves one click, with no extra risk</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It saves one approval, but the other side may use a very large allowance</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. An unlimited approval revokes itself the same day</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "An unlimited approval saves one confirmation, and if something goes wrong, a large amount of that coin can be moved. An unlimited approval lets the other side use a very large token allowance, without a new approval each time. It is more convenient, and it also magnifies the loss from a contract bug or a malicious permission. Read the scope of the permission first, then approve only the amount you need. Think it through What is the main difference between an unlimited approval and approving a specific amount each time? A. It only saves one click, with no extra risk B. It saves one approval, but the other side may use a very large allowance C. An unlimited approval revokes itself the same day"}, {"title": "Common scams", "html": "<p><strong>If a stranger starts by asking you to sign, to pay, or to hand over a seed phrase, treat it as a scam first.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fake airdrop: it asks you to sign, approve, or pay a fee first.</li>\n<li>Fake support: they message you first, then ask you to install remote software or hand over recovery information.</li>\n<li>Fake investment group: they show profit screenshots and ask you to send funds to a named platform.</li>\n<li>Cannot withdraw: they say you must pay more tax, an unfreeze fee, or a deposit before you can withdraw.</li>\n<li>Fake recovery: they claim stolen funds can definitely be recovered, and they want to be paid first.</li>\n<li>Address poisoning: a lookalike address is placed in your history so you will copy it.</li>\n<li>Malicious tokens and NFTs: the name or the note contains a claim link, and it pushes you to open it.</li>\n<li>Clipboard hijack: malware replaces the address you just copied with someone else's address.</li>\n<li>A third party reading the clipboard: an app or extension may read recovery information, a password, or a key you copied. Permissions and prompts differ by system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A strange asset showing up in the wallet does not mean you have to do something with it.<br />\nDo not open a link inside it just because the page shows a high value.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A strange asset can sit there. A high displayed value is not a reason to open the link.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not open the link in the note, and do not approve it just to see a balance. A strange asset can be left alone.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>A strange coin suddenly appears in the wallet, shows a high value, and includes a claim link. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Open the link and claim the coin</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Leave it alone, and do not open the link just because the value looks high</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Give that coin an unlimited approval immediately, or you cannot see the real balance</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If a stranger starts by asking you to sign, to pay, or to hand over a seed phrase, treat it as a scam first. Fake airdrop: it asks you to sign, approve, or pay a fee first. Fake support: they message you first, then ask you to install remote software or hand over recovery information. Fake investment group: they show profit screenshots and ask you to send funds to a named platform. Cannot withdraw: they say you must pay more tax, an unfreeze fee, or a deposit before you can withdraw. Fake recovery: they claim stolen funds can definitely be recovered, and they want to be paid first. Address poisoning: a lookalike address is placed in your history so you will copy it. Malicious tokens and NFTs: the name or the note contains a claim link, and it pushes you to open it. Clipboard hijack: malware replaces the address you just copied with someone else's address. A third party reading the clipboard: an app or extension may read recovery information, a password, or a key you copied. Permissions and prompts differ by system. A strange asset showing up in the wallet does not mean you have to do something with it. Do not open a link inside it just because the page shows a high value. Think it through A strange coin suddenly appears in the wallet, shows a high value, and includes a claim link. What should you do? A. Open the link and claim the coin B. Leave it alone, and do not open the link just because the value looks high C. Give that coin an unlimited approval immediately, or you cannot see the real balance"}, {"title": "Three traps that show up constantly in crypto", "html": "<p><strong>Dirty-money freezes, pig-butchering scams, and letting someone use your account to collect funds are three of the most common traps.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. Dirty money and a frozen card</strong></p>\n<p>In a P2P trade, if the money you receive has a dirty source, the bank card can be frozen, and you can be pulled into a case.<br />\nTrade only with merchants the platform has verified.<br />\nDo not take a payment from a third party, and do not collect money for someone else.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Pig-butchering</strong></p>\n<p>The other person starts with a personal relationship, or pulls you into a \"signals group\", and then steers you to deposit on a platform.<br />\nAt first you see a small profit. Once the amount is larger, you cannot withdraw.<br />\nIf someone you met online offers to invest for you, treat it as a scam first.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Buying your coins at a premium, and running funds through your account</strong></p>\n<p>Someone offers a high price for your coins, or asks to borrow your bank card or account to receive and forward money.<br />\nThat can be using your account to move money from a bad source.<br />\nHelping move funds, or lending a bank card to receive coins, can be the crime of assisting a criminal information network.<br />\nIn a serious case it can also be concealing or hiding criminal proceeds.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A premium offer for your coins, plus lending a bank card to receive funds, is not a normal trade. It can be the crime of assisting a criminal network, and in a serious case it can involve concealing criminal proceeds.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. This is not a normal P2P trade with a verified merchant, and it is not a risk-free favor. Lending an account to receive funds can be illegal.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone offers a high price for your coins and also asks to borrow your bank card to receive the money. Which situation is this closer to?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. A normal P2P trade with a merchant the platform verified</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The risk of letting someone run funds through your account. Lending an account to receive money can involve assisting a crime</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Just helping a friend, with no legal risk</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Dirty-money freezes, pig-butchering scams, and letting someone use your account to collect funds are three of the most common traps. 1. Dirty money and a frozen card In a P2P trade, if the money you receive has a dirty source, the bank card can be frozen, and you can be pulled into a case. Trade only with merchants the platform has verified. Do not take a payment from a third party, and do not collect money for someone else. 2. Pig-butchering The other person starts with a personal relationship, or pulls you into a \"signals group\", and then steers you to deposit on a platform. At first you see a small profit. Once the amount is larger, you cannot withdraw. If someone you met online offers to invest for you, treat it as a scam first. 3. Buying your coins at a premium, and running funds through your account Someone offers a high price for your coins, or asks to borrow your bank card or account to receive and forward money. That can be using your account to move money from a bad source. Helping move funds, or lending a bank card to receive coins, can be the crime of assisting a criminal information network. In a serious case it can also be concealing or hiding criminal proceeds. Think it through Someone offers a high price for your coins and also asks to borrow your bank card to receive the money. Which situation is this closer to? A. A normal P2P trade with a merchant the platform verified B. The risk of letting someone run funds through your account. Lending an account to receive money can involve assisting a crime C. Just helping a friend, with no legal risk"}, {"title": "Stop the clipboard from leaking", "html": "<p><strong>Do not copy a seed phrase or a private key, and do not paste one into any chat or web page.</strong></p>\n<p>Do not copy a seed phrase, a private key, or an API key onto the clipboard.<br />\nDo not paste them into a chat, an AI, an online note, or a web page.<br />\nFor passwords, prefer autofill from a password manager you trust.</p>\n<p>Remove apps, keyboards, and browser extensions you do not need or cannot trace.<br />\nCheck clipboard, accessibility, and device permissions.<br />\nTurn off clipboard history, cloud sync, and sharing across devices when you do not need them.<br />\nKeep the system updated.</p>\n<p>Clearing the clipboard only reduces the chance it will be read again later.<br />\nIt cannot take back something that has already been read.<br />\nIf you suspect a secret has leaked, update the matching credential.<br />\nIf a seed phrase or private key has leaked, handle it as in Chapter 15.</p>\n<p>For how to guard against a replaced address, see Chapter 10, \"Four checks before you send\".</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Clearing the clipboard cannot take back something that was already read. A leaked seed phrase has to be handled as in Chapter 15.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Clearing it cannot undo a leak that already happened. Do not paste the seed phrase into a chat or a web page again.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Someone already read the seed phrase from the clipboard. If you clear the clipboard now, does that undo the leak?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Clearing it means it never leaked</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Clearing it only reduces the chance it will be read again later</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Paste the seed phrase into a chat once more, to check it, and that is safer</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Do not copy a seed phrase or a private key, and do not paste one into any chat or web page. Do not copy a seed phrase, a private key, or an API key onto the clipboard. Do not paste them into a chat, an AI, an online note, or a web page. For passwords, prefer autofill from a password manager you trust. Remove apps, keyboards, and browser extensions you do not need or cannot trace. Check clipboard, accessibility, and device permissions. Turn off clipboard history, cloud sync, and sharing across devices when you do not need them. Keep the system updated. Clearing the clipboard only reduces the chance it will be read again later. It cannot take back something that has already been read. If you suspect a secret has leaked, update the matching credential. If a seed phrase or private key has leaked, handle it as in Chapter 15. For how to guard against a replaced address, see Chapter 10, \"Four checks before you send\". Think it through Someone already read the seed phrase from the clipboard. If you clear the clipboard now, does that undo the leak? A. Yes. Clearing it means it never leaked B. No. Clearing it only reduces the chance it will be read again later C. Paste the seed phrase into a chat once more, to check it, and that is safer"}, {"title": "Protect the account and the device", "html": "<p><strong>If the email and the phone are held, the account has lost most of its front doors.</strong></p>\n<p>Protect the email.<br />\nUse a password that is not reused, and turn on a reliable second factor.<br />\nUpdate the system and the wallet.<br />\nDo not install a plugin you cannot trace.<br />\nDo not show recovery information on a shared screen.<br />\nIf the exchange offers a withdrawal allowlist and login alerts, turn them on.</p>\n<p>An API key is a secret too.<br />\nIf you automate later, use the smallest permission.<br />\nA trading bot usually does not need withdrawal permission.<br />\nDo not send the key to a stranger's bot, and do not publish it in a code repository.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A trading bot usually does not need withdrawal permission. Do not give the key to a stranger's bot, and do not publish it in a repository.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not turn on withdrawal permission just to save trouble, and do not publish the key or give it to a stranger's bot.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Does an API key used by a trading bot usually need withdrawal permission turned on?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, or the bot cannot be used</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Usually not. Use the smallest permission, and do not send the key to a stranger's bot</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Publish the key in a code repository, so you have a backup</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If the email and the phone are held, the account has lost most of its front doors. Protect the email. Use a password that is not reused, and turn on a reliable second factor. Update the system and the wallet. Do not install a plugin you cannot trace. Do not show recovery information on a shared screen. If the exchange offers a withdrawal allowlist and login alerts, turn them on. An API key is a secret too. If you automate later, use the smallest permission. A trading bot usually does not need withdrawal permission. Do not send the key to a stranger's bot, and do not publish it in a code repository. Think it through Does an API key used by a trading bot usually need withdrawal permission turned on? A. Yes, or the bot cannot be used B. Usually not. Use the smallest permission, and do not send the key to a stranger's bot C. Publish the key in a code repository, so you have a backup"}, {"title": "Privacy needs protecting too", "html": "<p><strong>Many people can look up an on-chain transfer, and a wallet that does not show a real name does not mean you cannot be found.</strong></p>\n<p>Transactions on many public chains can be looked up in public.<br />\nA public address can be tied to a balance, to habits, and to an identity.<br />\nThe wallet screen not showing a real name does not mean the record cannot be traced.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. No real name on the screen does not mean the on-chain record is private. A public address can still be tied to behavior and to a person.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Many records on a public chain can be looked up. A wallet that does not show a real name is not the same as being untraceable.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>The wallet does not show a real name. Does that mean an on-chain transfer cannot be traced?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. No real name means it cannot be found</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. The record can be looked up in public, and the address can still be tied to a person</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Only an exchange account's records can be seen by anyone else</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Many people can look up an on-chain transfer, and a wallet that does not show a real name does not mean you cannot be found. Transactions on many public chains can be looked up in public. A public address can be tied to a balance, to habits, and to an identity. The wallet screen not showing a real name does not mean the record cannot be traced. Note: Safety is not just one cold wallet. It is a set of habits you keep. Think it through The wallet does not show a real name. Does that mean an on-chain transfer cannot be traced? A. Yes. No real name means it cannot be found B. No. The record can be looked up in public, and the address can still be tied to a person C. Only an exchange account's records can be seen by anyone else"}]}, {"id": "10", "title": "Your first on-chain actions", "emoji": "🔗", "intro": "Receive, send, gas, swaps, and bridges", "tags": ["How-to"], "tip": "Note: For a first on-chain practice, one correct small receive and one correct small send matter more than rushing to bridge.", "sections": [{"title": "Receiving", "html": "<p><strong>To receive, you give an address and a network. You do not give a seed phrase.</strong></p>\n<p>Open a wallet or platform you trust. Select the correct asset and network. Copy the receiving address. If a Memo / Tag is required, give that too. Confirm the sender supports that asset and that network.</p>\n<p>Do not give anyone a seed phrase in order to receive funds. Receiving usually needs only the public receiving details.</p>", "text": "To receive, you give an address and a network. You do not give a seed phrase. Open a wallet or platform you trust. Select the correct asset and network. Copy the receiving address. If a Memo / Tag is required, give that too. Confirm the sender supports that asset and that network. Do not give anyone a seed phrase in order to receive funds. Receiving usually needs only the public receiving details."}, {"title": "Four checks before you send", "html": "<p><strong>Before you send, check four things: asset, network, address, and Memo.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Asset: the name matches the real contract or the real native asset.</li>\n<li>Network: the recipient explicitly supports the network you selected.</li>\n<li>Address: check the full address against a source you trust, so a swapped clipboard does not win. After you paste, check the pasted result, and check again on the wallet or exchange confirmation screen. The first and last few characters are not enough. A lookalike address can copy those on purpose.<br />\n   If you are unsure, you can scan a receiving QR code from a channel the recipient actually controls, which skips some copy-and-paste. After the scan, still check the full address, the network, and whether the code prefilled an amount or some other action. Scanning does not rule out a fake or malicious QR code.</li>\n<li>Memo / Tag: if the receiving platform requires it, it has to be correct.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p>📰 <strong>A real case: address poisoning, one wrong send of 68 million dollars (May 2024)</strong><br />\nThe scammer first sent the victim a tiny transfer, so a fake address with <strong>the same first 6 and last 6 characters as the real one</strong> landed in the transfer history. The victim copied that fake address and sent 1,155 WBTC (about 68 million dollars). After negotiation the scammer returned most of it. That outcome is extremely rare. In almost every other case the funds are not recovered.<br />\n<strong>The lesson: do not copy an address out of your transfer history. Check the full address every time.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Also check the minimum deposit, the fees, whether the network is paused, and how many confirmations are required. A small test transfer has to be above the relevant minimum. Move the rest only after it arrives.</p>", "text": "Before you send, check four things: asset, network, address, and Memo. Asset: the name matches the real contract or the real native asset. Network: the recipient explicitly supports the network you selected. Address: check the full address against a source you trust, so a swapped clipboard does not win. After you paste, check the pasted result, and check again on the wallet or exchange confirmation screen. The first and last few characters are not enough. A lookalike address can copy those on purpose. If you are unsure, you can scan a receiving QR code from a channel the recipient actually controls, which skips some copy-and-paste. After the scan, still check the full address, the network, and whether the code prefilled an amount or some other action. Scanning does not rule out a fake or malicious QR code. Memo / Tag: if the receiving platform requires it, it has to be correct. 📰 A real case: address poisoning, one wrong send of 68 million dollars (May 2024) The scammer first sent the victim a tiny transfer, so a fake address with the same first 6 and last 6 characters as the real one landed in the transfer history. The victim copied that fake address and sent 1,155 WBTC (about 68 million dollars). After negotiation the scammer returned most of it. That outcome is extremely rare. In almost every other case the funds are not recovered. The lesson: do not copy an address out of your transfer history. Check the full address every time. Also check the minimum deposit, the fees, whether the network is paused, and how many confirmations are required. A small test transfer has to be above the relevant minimum. Move the rest only after it arrives."}, {"title": "Why does the same coin have different networks?", "html": "<p><strong>USDT with the same name on a different network is a different thing. Do not pick the wrong one.</strong></p>\n<p>The name can be USDT,<br />\nand the network can still differ.</p>\n<p>Whichever network the recipient chose,<br />\nthat is the network you choose.</p>\n<p>Do not pick one<br />\nonly because the fee looks cheaper.</p>\n<p>An asset with the same name can be issued on different chains, and there are bridged or wrapped versions. The same name does not mean the same contract, the same issuer, or the same redemption path.</p>\n<p>TRON's TRC-20 and Ethereum's ERC-20 are token standards on different networks.</p>", "text": "USDT with the same name on a different network is a different thing. Do not pick the wrong one. The name can be USDT, and the network can still differ. Whichever network the recipient chose, that is the network you choose. Do not pick one only because the fee looks cheaper. An asset with the same name can be issued on different chains, and there are bridged or wrapped versions. The same name does not mean the same contract, the same issuer, or the same redemption path. TRON's TRC-20 and Ethereum's ERC-20 are token standards on different networks."}, {"title": "Address and contract address", "html": "<p><strong>A contract address is the token's identity. It is not your receiving address.</strong></p>\n<p>A receiving address is the account that receives funds. A contract address identifies a token or a contract. When you buy a token you may use the contract address to check identity. For an ordinary transfer, do not paste the token's contract address in as if it were your own receiving address.</p>", "text": "A contract address is the token's identity. It is not your receiving address. A receiving address is the account that receives funds. A contract address identifies a token or a contract. When you buy a token you may use the contract address to check identity. For an ordinary transfer, do not paste the token's contract address in as if it were your own receiving address."}, {"title": "Gas and network resources", "html": "<p><strong>Sending costs a network fee. Tokens with no gas beside them still cannot move.</strong></p>\n<h3>Why does the wallet show coins, and still refuse to send?</h3>\n<p>Sending also pays a \"fare\",<br />\nwhich is the network fee.</p>\n<p>To send USDT on Ethereum,<br />\nyou usually also need a little ETH for that fare.</p>\n<p>Some wallets can pay it for you.<br />\nRead the prompt in the wallet.</p>\n<p>A network fee is the cost of executing the transaction. Many chains pay it in the native asset: Ethereum commonly uses ETH, Solana commonly uses SOL, BNB Chain commonly uses BNB. TRON involves bandwidth, energy, and possibly TRX.</p>\n<p>Some wallets or apps support sponsored gas, resource rental, or another way to pay. The rules depend on that service.</p>", "text": "Sending costs a network fee. Tokens with no gas beside them still cannot move. Why does the wallet show coins, and still refuse to send? Sending also pays a \"fare\", which is the network fee. To send USDT on Ethereum, you usually also need a little ETH for that fare. Some wallets can pay it for you. Read the prompt in the wallet. A network fee is the cost of executing the transaction. Many chains pay it in the native asset: Ethereum commonly uses ETH, Solana commonly uses SOL, BNB Chain commonly uses BNB. TRON involves bandwidth, energy, and possibly TRX. Some wallets or apps support sponsored gas, resource rental, or another way to pay. The rules depend on that service."}, {"title": "How do you see whether a transaction went through?", "html": "<p><strong>Take the TxID to a block explorer. That tells you whether it succeeded.</strong></p>\n<p>A transaction hash / TxID is the identifier you use to look up one transaction. On the explorer for that network, check whether it succeeded, the receiving address, the asset, the fee, and the confirmations.</p>\n<p>Success on-chain and a credit on an exchange are two different events. The platform may also require a number of confirmations, a minimum amount, or manual processing.</p>", "text": "Take the TxID to a block explorer. That tells you whether it succeeded. A transaction hash / TxID is the identifier you use to look up one transaction. On the explorer for that network, check whether it succeeded, the receiving address, the asset, the fee, and the confirmations. Success on-chain and a credit on an exchange are two different events. The platform may also require a number of confirmations, a minimum amount, or manual processing."}, {"title": "Swap: exchanging on-chain", "html": "<p><strong>Before a swap, read how much you will receive and the slippage. Then sign.</strong></p>\n<p>A swap exchanges one asset for another. Confirm the token contract, the route, the amount you expect to receive, the price impact, the slippage, and the network fee before you decide to sign.</p>\n<p>On some protocols, approving and then swapping are two separate transactions. A successful approval is not a completed swap. Raising slippage does not make the trade safe. Set it too high and you accept a very bad fill.</p>", "text": "Before a swap, read how much you will receive and the slippage. Then sign. A swap exchanges one asset for another. Confirm the token contract, the route, the amount you expect to receive, the price impact, the slippage, and the network fee before you decide to sign. On some protocols, approving and then swapping are two separate transactions. A successful approval is not a completed swap. Raising slippage does not make the trade safe. Set it too high and you accept a very bad fill."}, {"title": "Bridges", "html": "<p><strong>A bridge is riskier than an ordinary transfer. Do not rush one on your first try.</strong></p>\n<p>A bridge moves assets or messages between networks. After the bridge, the asset may be a wrapped or mapped version. You need to understand who you are trusting and how redemption works.</p>\n<p>Check the source chain, the destination chain, the bridge protocol, the asset version, the fees, the wait, and gas on the destination chain. A bridge can involve contract risk, the verification mechanism, custody, liquidity, or the front end.</p>\n", "text": "A bridge is riskier than an ordinary transfer. Do not rush one on your first try. A bridge moves assets or messages between networks. After the bridge, the asset may be a wrapped or mapped version. You need to understand who you are trusting and how redemption works. Check the source chain, the destination chain, the bridge protocol, the asset version, the fees, the wait, and gas on the destination chain. A bridge can involve contract risk, the verification mechanism, custody, liquidity, or the front end. Note: For a first on-chain practice, one correct small receive and one correct small send matter more than rushing to bridge."}]}, {"id": "11", "title": "How to research a project", "emoji": "🔍", "intro": "Use, token, data, and risk", "tags": ["Advanced"], "tip": "Note: Not understanding it is a reason to keep reading. It is not a reason to buy because you are afraid of missing it.", "sections": [{"title": "Question 1: what does it do?", "html": "<p><strong>If you cannot say what the project does in one sentence, do not buy it yet.</strong></p>\n<p>Say, in one sentence, the problem it works on, who it is for, and what the product is. If all you can repeat is \"revolutionary\" and \"empowering the ecosystem\", and you cannot say how anyone uses it, keep researching.</p>", "text": "If you cannot say what the project does in one sentence, do not buy it yet. Say, in one sentence, the problem it works on, who it is for, and what the product is. If all you can repeat is \"revolutionary\" and \"empowering the ecosystem\", and you cannot say how anyone uses it, keep researching."}, {"title": "Question 2: what is the coin for?", "html": "<p><strong>A good project is not the same as a useful coin. Ask what the coin actually does.</strong></p>\n<p>A good product does not mean the token captures value. Check what the token is for, how supply and demand work, what rights holders actually have, where fees go, and who governs.</p>\n<p>\"Platform revenue is growing\" does not automatically mean that revenue is paid to token holders. A buyback plan also has to be checked: is it actually carried out, and can it continue?</p>", "text": "A good project is not the same as a useful coin. Ask what the coin actually does. A good product does not mean the token captures value. Check what the token is for, how supply and demand work, what rights holders actually have, where fees go, and who governs. \"Platform revenue is growing\" does not automatically mean that revenue is paid to token holders. A buyback plan also has to be checked: is it actually carried out, and can it continue?"}, {"title": "Question 3: who holds the coins, and when can they sell?", "html": "<p><strong>See who holds the coins and when they unlock, so you are not the one left under a dump.</strong></p>\n<p>Look at allocations to the team, investors, the community, and the treasury, the share already circulating, and the unlock schedule. Concentration of holdings has to be read with who the address is: an exchange's pooled address is not necessarily one whale.</p>", "text": "See who holds the coins and when they unlock, so you are not the one left under a dump. Look at allocations to the team, investors, the community, and the treasury, the share already circulating, and the unlock schedule. Concentration of holdings has to be read with who the address is: an exchange's pooled address is not necessarily one whale."}, {"title": "Extra check: which exchanges already list it?", "html": "<p><strong>Whether it is listed is decided by the exchange's own announcement.</strong></p>\n<p>Check the exchange's official asset page and announcements. Do not stop at the project's poster. Check the contract, the network, the pair, and whether trading, deposits, and withdrawals are actually open.</p>\n<p>Separate a spot listing, a contract listing, pre-market trading, and a token that some wallet's DEX aggregator happens to show. A token searchable in a brand's wallet is not the same as that brand's CEX having officially listed the spot market. Also check for recent delistings, pauses, or restriction notices.</p>", "text": "Whether it is listed is decided by the exchange's own announcement. Check the exchange's official asset page and announcements. Do not stop at the project's poster. Check the contract, the network, the pair, and whether trading, deposits, and withdrawals are actually open. Separate a spot listing, a contract listing, pre-market trading, and a token that some wallet's DEX aggregator happens to show. A token searchable in a brand's wallet is not the same as that brand's CEX having officially listed the spot market. Also check for recent delistings, pauses, or restriction notices."}, {"title": "If it is new: who led the round, and which institutions are in?", "html": "<p><strong>An institutional investment can be checked. It does not mean the price will rise.</strong></p>\n<p>Look up the round, the date, the amount that was made public, the lead, and the other investors. Cross-check announcements from the project and from the institution itself. Equity investment, a token investment, a grant, a technical partnership, and simply showing up at an event are not the same relationship.</p>\n<p>\"Institutional backing\" is best written as a fact you can verify, for example \"this institution joined this round\". Do not treat logos on a website as proof of a partnership. Do not treat an investor's name as a promise about the future price.</p>", "text": "An institutional investment can be checked. It does not mean the price will rise. Look up the round, the date, the amount that was made public, the lead, and the other investors. Cross-check announcements from the project and from the institution itself. Equity investment, a token investment, a grant, a technical partnership, and simply showing up at an event are not the same relationship. \"Institutional backing\" is best written as a fact you can verify, for example \"this institution joined this round\". Do not treat logos on a website as proof of a partnership. Do not treat an investor's name as a promise about the future price."}, {"title": "Question 4: what do the numbers say?", "html": "<p><strong>Before you trust a number, ask how it was counted. A marketing screenshot needs its original source.</strong></p>\n<p>TVL is the value of locked assets under a specific set of rules. The coin price and double counting can move it.<br />\nActive addresses are not the same as distinct real people.<br />\nVolume is not revenue.<br />\nProtocol fees, protocol revenue, and net profit are not the same metric.</p>\n<p>Before you compare, check the date, the unit, the time range, and the definition. For a screenshot in a pitch, try to find the original source.</p>", "text": "Before you trust a number, ask how it was counted. A marketing screenshot needs its original source. TVL is the value of locked assets under a specific set of rules. The coin price and double counting can move it. Active addresses are not the same as distinct real people. Volume is not revenue. Protocol fees, protocol revenue, and net profit are not the same metric. Before you compare, check the date, the unit, the time range, and the definition. For a screenshot in a pitch, try to find the original source."}, {"title": "Question 5: what powers exist, and how do you get out?", "html": "<p><strong>Check who can mint, freeze, or change the rules before you consider buying.</strong></p>\n<p>Who can upgrade the contract, pause it, mint more, freeze, or change fees? Can you sell normally? Is there a transfer tax, a blacklist, or a transfer restriction? Who controls the pool's liquidity?</p>\n<p>A safety scanner can only offer some clues. It cannot guarantee there is no malicious logic. A small test buy and sell also cannot prove you will be able to exit later.</p>", "text": "Check who can mint, freeze, or change the rules before you consider buying. Who can upgrade the contract, pause it, mint more, freeze, or change fees? Can you sell normally? Is there a transfer tax, a blacklist, or a transfer restriction? Who controls the pool's liquidity? A safety scanner can only offer some clues. It cannot guarantee there is no malicious logic. A small test buy and sell also cannot prove you will be able to exit later."}, {"title": "What does an audit mean?", "html": "<p><strong>An audit means someone reviewed a specific version of the code. It is not a promise of no bugs.</strong></p>\n<p>An audit inspects specific code and a specific scope. It may find problems. It is not proof that there are no bugs. Read what was audited, the version, the date, the issues still open, and whether the code that shipped matches the version that was audited.</p>", "text": "An audit means someone reviewed a specific version of the code. It is not a promise of no bugs. An audit inspects specific code and a specific scope. It may find problems. It is not proof that there are no bugs. Read what was audited, the version, the date, the issues still open, and whether the code that shipped matches the version that was audited."}, {"title": "Sources, and who is being paid to talk", "html": "<p><strong>Official docs and on-chain data come first. KOLs come after, and notice whether they are paid.</strong></p>\n<p>Read official docs, on-chain records, and independent data before community chatter. Fundraising, listings, celebrity attention, and partnership announcements all need checking. None of them is a promise of profit.</p>\n<p>When you read a KOL, notice whether they hold the asset, whether they are paid to promote it, or whether they use a referral link. Those interests change what they emphasize.</p>", "text": "Official docs and on-chain data come first. KOLs come after, and notice whether they are paid. Read official docs, on-chain records, and independent data before community chatter. Fundraising, listings, celebrity attention, and partnership announcements all need checking. None of them is a promise of profit. When you read a KOL, notice whether they hold the asset, whether they are paid to promote it, or whether they use a referral link. Those interests change what they emphasize."}, {"title": "A one-page research note", "html": "<p><strong>Write the research on one page. If you cannot, you have not thought it through.</strong></p>\n<p>Project name and official entrance:<br />\nProduct and users:<br />\nWhat the token is for:<br />\nCirculation, valuation, and unlocks:<br />\nContract and admin powers:<br />\nData you can verify, and the date:<br />\nMain risks and how you would exit:<br />\nWhat I still do not understand:</p>\n", "text": "Write the research on one page. If you cannot, you have not thought it through. Project name and official entrance: Product and users: What the token is for: Circulation, valuation, and unlocks: Contract and admin powers: Data you can verify, and the date: Main risks and how you would exit: What I still do not understand: Note: Not understanding it is a reason to keep reading. It is not a reason to buy because you are afraid of missing it."}]}, {"id": "12", "title": "A map of Web3 activities", "emoji": "🎮", "intro": "DeFi, airdrops, NFTs, memes, and RWAs", "tags": ["Advanced"], "tip": "Note: For every activity, ask four things: what it is, how you take part, what it costs, and how you can lose.", "sections": [{"title": "First you need a wallet: the start of every activity 👛", "html": "<p><strong>DeFi, airdrops, NFTs, memes: the first step for all of them is an on-chain wallet.</strong></p>\n<p>Every activity below needs an on-chain wallet to connect to an app, to sign, and to hold the assets. An exchange account cannot stand in for it.</p>\n<p>Beginners can start with Binance Wallet (keyless). It is easier at the start:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You do not write down a traditional seed phrase.</li>\n<li>It uses MPC. The key is split into 3 shares. Any two are enough to use it, and Binance alone cannot move your money.</li>\n<li>Before a transfer it can show a risk reminder, which is one more check.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>💬 From the person who runs this site: I once nearly sent money to a contract address. Binance Wallet popped up a warning and stopped me. For a beginner, that kind of reminder really helps.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For how it works and how to back it up, see <a href=\"#/c/08/4\">Chapter 08, &quot;A keyless wallet beginners can start with&quot;</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Binance Wallet (keyless)</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://web3.binance.com/referral?ref=0XMAY\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>A self-custody on-chain wallet that suits a beginner. You can connect DApps, sign, and hold on-chain assets. It is not the same thing as the exchange account.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>web3.binance.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://web3.binance.com/referral?ref=0XMAY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://web3.binance.com/referral?ref=0XMAY\">Open ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, web3.binance.com)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "DeFi, airdrops, NFTs, memes: the first step for all of them is an on-chain wallet. Every activity below needs an on-chain wallet to connect to an app, to sign, and to hold the assets. An exchange account cannot stand in for it. Beginners can start with Binance Wallet (keyless). It is easier at the start: You do not write down a traditional seed phrase. It uses MPC. The key is split into 3 shares. Any two are enough to use it, and Binance alone cannot move your money. Before a transfer it can show a risk reminder, which is one more check. 💬 From the person who runs this site: I once nearly sent money to a contract address. Binance Wallet popped up a warning and stopped me. For a beginner, that kind of reminder really helps. For how it works and how to back it up, see Chapter 08, \"A keyless wallet beginners can start with\" . Binance Wallet (keyless) ⏳ Not checked yet A self-custody on-chain wallet that suits a beginner. You can connect DApps, sign, and hold on-chain assets. It is not the same thing as the exchange account. Domain: web3.binance.com Open ↗ (new window, web3.binance.com)"}, {"title": "DeFi: financial apps on a chain", "html": "<p><strong>DeFi is banking and exchange functions on a chain. There is no support desk to catch you.</strong></p>\n<p>DeFi means apps that use smart contracts for swaps, lending, and similar financial functions. You usually interact with a wallet. Costs include network fees and protocol fees. Risk can come from the code, the collateral, oracles, governance powers, and the economic design.</p>", "text": "DeFi is banking and exchange functions on a chain. There is no support desk to catch you. DeFi means apps that use smart contracts for swaps, lending, and similar financial functions. You usually interact with a wallet. Costs include network fees and protocol fees. Risk can come from the code, the collateral, oracles, governance powers, and the economic design."}, {"title": "Lending", "html": "<p><strong>Lending is about collateral and liquidation. Do not look only at the yield.</strong></p>\n<p>Depositing an asset may earn a return. Borrowing an asset requires collateral and interest. If the collateral price falls, you can be liquidated. The deposit side also has bad-debt, liquidity, and contract risk. Do not look only at the yield.</p>", "text": "Lending is about collateral and liquidation. Do not look only at the yield. Depositing an asset may earn a return. Borrowing an asset requires collateral and interest. If the collateral price falls, you can be liquidated. The deposit side also has bad-debt, liquidity, and contract risk. Do not look only at the yield."}, {"title": "Liquidity pools", "html": "<p><strong>Providing liquidity earns fees, and you also take impermanent loss.</strong></p>\n<p>You put assets into a pool and may earn trading fees or rewards. Besides the assets' own price moves, there is impermanent loss, rewards that lose value, and contract risk. Impermanent loss describes the value difference versus simply holding the assets. It is not guaranteed to \"come back\".</p>", "text": "Providing liquidity earns fees, and you also take impermanent loss. You put assets into a pool and may earn trading fees or rewards. Besides the assets' own price moves, there is impermanent loss, rewards that lose value, and contract risk. Impermanent loss describes the value difference versus simply holding the assets. It is not guaranteed to \"come back\"."}, {"title": "Staking and yield products", "html": "<p><strong>If a product is called \"earn\", first ask what the money is actually doing.</strong></p>\n<p>Native staking on a proof-of-stake network supports validation and pays rewards. There can be an exit wait, validator risk, or a penalty mechanism.</p>\n<p>A product a platform labels \"staking\" or \"Earn\" may actually be lending or some other yield strategy. You have to read what the funds are used for, the redemption rules, and who bears the risk. The product name is not enough.</p>\n<p>APR is an annualized rate without compounding. APY includes a compounding assumption. An annualized number on the screen is not a guaranteed return for the next year.</p>", "text": "If a product is called \"earn\", first ask what the money is actually doing. Native staking on a proof-of-stake network supports validation and pays rewards. There can be an exit wait, validator risk, or a penalty mechanism. A product a platform labels \"staking\" or \"Earn\" may actually be lending or some other yield strategy. You have to read what the funds are used for, the redemption rules, and who bears the risk. The product name is not enough. APR is an annualized rate without compounding. APY includes a compounding assumption. An annualized number on the screen is not a guaranteed return for the next year."}, {"title": "Airdrops, points, and testnets", "html": "<p><strong>Airdrops and points do not guarantee a payoff. Do not approve things blindly for them.</strong></p>\n<p>You may get a chance at a future distribution by using a product or joining an activity. The cost includes time, network fees, and interaction risk. Points do not guarantee a token redemption. Test assets usually have no mainnet value, and there may be no reward.</p>", "text": "Airdrops and points do not guarantee a payoff. Do not approve things blindly for them. You may get a chance at a future distribution by using a product or joining an activity. The cost includes time, network fees, and interaction risk. Points do not guarantee a token redemption. Test assets usually have no mainnet value, and there may be no reward."}, {"title": "NFTs", "html": "<p><strong>Buying an NFT does not mean you bought the copyright to the picture.</strong></p>\n<p>An NFT is a token used to represent a unique identifier and related information. Buying an NFT does not automatically give you copyright in an image or other legal rights. The actual rights depend on the license and the terms. Price, liquidity, metadata, and whether the project continues are all risks.</p>", "text": "Buying an NFT does not mean you bought the copyright to the picture. An NFT is a token used to represent a unique identifier and related information. Buying an NFT does not automatically give you copyright in an image or other legal rights. The actual rights depend on the license and the terms. Price, liquidity, metadata, and whether the project continues are all risks."}, {"title": "On-chain games and DAOs", "html": "<p><strong>A game lasts if it is fun to play. Holding a governance token is not the same as being a shareholder.</strong></p>\n<p>An on-chain game puts some game assets or mechanics on a chain. Whether the game is fun, and whether the token economics can last, are separate questions.<br />\nA DAO is a way of organizing people with rules and a governance mechanism. Holding a governance token does not mean you own equity in a company. Governance power can also be highly concentrated.</p>", "text": "A game lasts if it is fun to play. Holding a governance token is not the same as being a shareholder. An on-chain game puts some game assets or mechanics on a chain. Whether the game is fun, and whether the token economics can last, are separate questions. A DAO is a way of organizing people with rules and a governance mechanism. Holding a governance token does not mean you own equity in a company. Governance power can also be highly concentrated."}, {"title": "Meme coins", "html": "<p><strong>A meme coin can surge, and it can go to zero. Only use money you can afford to lose.</strong></p>\n<p>Meme coins often grow around a culture, a community, and how the story spreads. They can rise by astonishing amounts, and a few participants make a lot. They can also go to zero quickly, or become impossible to sell, and participants lose most or all of the principal.</p>\n<p>People often compare buying a meme coin to \"buying a lottery ticket\", because the outcome is highly uncertain and it is easy to see only the get-rich stories. It does not have a lottery's fixed, transparent prize mechanism. Concentrated holdings, insider advantage, bots front-running, malicious contracts, and liquidity also change the result.</p>\n<p>So you can understand the upside, and you also have to hold the chance of going to zero, of being unable to sell, and of uneven information in the same picture. Do not treat a get-rich story as the expected result for an ordinary participant.</p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Meme guide (DeBot)</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://debot.ai/r/0xMay\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>A tool for on-chain meme-coin activity and trading data. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token is safe. Before you join, accept that it may go to zero.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>debot.ai</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://debot.ai/r/0xMay\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://debot.ai/r/0xMay\">Open ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, debot.ai)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "A meme coin can surge, and it can go to zero. Only use money you can afford to lose. Meme coins often grow around a culture, a community, and how the story spreads. They can rise by astonishing amounts, and a few participants make a lot. They can also go to zero quickly, or become impossible to sell, and participants lose most or all of the principal. People often compare buying a meme coin to \"buying a lottery ticket\", because the outcome is highly uncertain and it is easy to see only the get-rich stories. It does not have a lottery's fixed, transparent prize mechanism. Concentrated holdings, insider advantage, bots front-running, malicious contracts, and liquidity also change the result. So you can understand the upside, and you also have to hold the chance of going to zero, of being unable to sell, and of uneven information in the same picture. Do not treat a get-rich story as the expected result for an ordinary participant. Meme guide (DeBot) ⏳ Not checked yet A tool for on-chain meme-coin activity and trading data. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token is safe. Before you join, accept that it may go to zero. Domain: debot.ai Open ↗ (new window, debot.ai)"}, {"title": "What is a prediction market? 🔮", "html": "<p><strong>A prediction market is trading on the outcome of a question.</strong></p>\n<p>For example:<br />\n\"Will this team win?\"<br />\n\"Will this product ship before the end of the year?\"</p>\n<p>You can buy \"yes\" or \"no\". If you are right, you are paid under the platform's rules. If you are wrong, the share you bought can go to zero.</p>\n<p>Believing the event will happen does not mean buying in is guaranteed to make money. The price you pay, the fees, and the rules that decide the outcome all change the result.</p>\n<p>Before you join, read:<br />\nwhen it closes, what counts as \"it happened\", and who decides.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ These products can lose money. In some places they may fall under gambling or financial rules. Check the rules where you are before you use one.</p>\n</blockquote>", "text": "A prediction market is trading on the outcome of a question. For example: \"Will this team win?\" \"Will this product ship before the end of the year?\" You can buy \"yes\" or \"no\". If you are right, you are paid under the platform's rules. If you are wrong, the share you bought can go to zero. Believing the event will happen does not mean buying in is guaranteed to make money. The price you pay, the fees, and the rules that decide the outcome all change the result. Before you join, read: when it closes, what counts as \"it happened\", and who decides. ⚠️ These products can lose money. In some places they may fall under gambling or financial rules. Check the rules where you are before you use one."}, {"title": "RWAs and tokenized stocks", "html": "<p><strong>A tokenized stock is not the same as you actually holding the stock.</strong></p>\n<p>RWA means an on-chain representation of a claim related to a real-world asset. A tokenized stock gives some exposure to a stock's price, or some related right. It is not the same as being the registered holder of the stock.</p>\n<p>Check the issuing entity, custody, the legal right, redemption, dividends, who is allowed to participate, and trading hours. A name that looks like a stock does not mean the rights are the same.</p>", "text": "A tokenized stock is not the same as you actually holding the stock. RWA means an on-chain representation of a claim related to a real-world asset. A tokenized stock gives some exposure to a stock's price, or some related right. It is not the same as being the registered holder of the stock. Check the issuing entity, custody, the legal right, redemption, dividends, who is allowed to participate, and trading hours. A name that looks like a stock does not mean the rights are the same."}, {"title": "DePIN and AI", "html": "<p><strong>For DePIN and AI projects, first look for real demand and real revenue.</strong></p>\n<p>DePIN usually means using a network and incentives to organize real-world infrastructure, such as compute, communications, or sensors. An AI and Web3 project may involve compute, data, or automated agents.</p>\n<p>Check real demand, hardware cost, where revenue comes from, and how permissions are set. If an AI agent can operate a wallet, be explicit about how much it may spend, what it can call, and how you revoke that permission.</p>\n", "text": "For DePIN and AI projects, first look for real demand and real revenue. DePIN usually means using a network and incentives to organize real-world infrastructure, such as compute, communications, or sensors. An AI and Web3 project may involve compute, data, or automated agents. Check real demand, hardware cost, where revenue comes from, and how permissions are set. If an AI agent can operate a wallet, be explicit about how much it may spend, what it can call, and how you revoke that permission. Note: For every activity, ask four things: what it is, how you take part, what it costs, and how you can lose."}]}, {"id": "13", "title": "Crypto slang translator", "emoji": "💬", "intro": "Serious terms and community jokes, kept apart", "tags": ["Glossary"], "sections": [{"title": "Serious terms", "html": "<p><strong>Serious terms are community shorthand, and you need the meaning before you can tell a slogan from a fact.</strong></p>\n<p>The table below lists common words in their existing wording.<br />\nKnowing the meaning is so you can understand what other people are saying.<br />\nIt does not turn a slogan into a fact.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DYOR</td>\n<td>Do your own research and check it yourself. Do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FOMO</td>\n<td>Fear of missing out, which pushes you to jump in on impulse.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FUD</td>\n<td>Fear, uncertainty, and doubt. The word cannot be used to dismiss every criticism.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HODL</td>\n<td>Community slang for holding for a long time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ATH / ATL</td>\n<td>All-time high / all-time low. Pay attention to the range of the data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bull / Bear</td>\n<td>Bullish / bearish. Also used for a bull market / a bear market.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whale</td>\n<td>A participant with a large holding or a large trade.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Degen</td>\n<td>Someone aggressive about high-risk plays. Often said with self-mockery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rug Pull</td>\n<td>The project side abandons participants by pulling funds or by some other exit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honeypot</td>\n<td>A trap that looks buyable but may block selling or transferring.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airdrop</td>\n<td>Assets allocated to users or addresses that qualify.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Snapshot</td>\n<td>A record of state at one moment in time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vesting</td>\n<td>Assets released gradually over time or under conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TGE</td>\n<td>Token generation event. Whether it circulates immediately depends on the rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whitepaper</td>\n<td>The project's write-up of its design and plan. It is not a promise that any of it will happen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tokenomics</td>\n<td>The token's economics: issuance, allocation, use, and incentives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TVL</td>\n<td>Total value locked. You need the scope and the definition behind the number.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FDV</td>\n<td>Fully diluted valuation. You need the supply definition behind it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gas</td>\n<td>The usual name for the network resources or the fee a transaction needs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TxID</td>\n<td>The transaction id / hash, used to look the transaction up.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approve</td>\n<td>An approval that lets a named party use an asset under the rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revoke</td>\n<td>Cancel that approval. It cannot recover assets already lost.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slippage</td>\n<td>The difference between the price you expected and the price you got.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LP</td>\n<td>Liquidity provider. Also often used for the liquidity position itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MEV</td>\n<td>Maximal extractable value: opportunities from ordering, including, or excluding transactions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CEX / DEX</td>\n<td>Centralized exchange / decentralized trading protocol or venue.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KYC</td>\n<td>The identity-verification process.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P2P</td>\n<td>A user-to-user trade. In crypto it often means buying and selling with fiat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long / Short</td>\n<td>Betting on a rise / betting on a fall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Funding Rate</td>\n<td>The funding rate on a perpetual contract.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquidation</td>\n<td>Forced close-out. The exact meaning depends on the setting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GM / GN</td>\n<td>Community greetings: good morning / good night.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>庄家 / 市场操纵 (market maker in the slang sense / manipulation)</td>\n<td>Casually called 狗庄. A person or group holding enough of a coin to move its price or its volume in an obvious way, for example by concentrated buying and selling, wash trading, or pumping and then distributing. Small-cap, illiquid coins are easier to push around.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meme coin</td>\n<td>A token built around a culture, a community, and how it spreads. The small, high-risk ones are often called 土狗. They can surge, and they can go to zero or become impossible to sell.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Swap</td>\n<td>Exchange one asset for another. Watch price impact, slippage, and the network fee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private Key</td>\n<td>The secret that authorizes actions on an account. Like a seed phrase, never give it to anyone.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MPC wallet</td>\n<td>A multi-party computation wallet. The key is split into shares kept apart. Creating one usually does not mean writing down a traditional seed phrase (Binance Wallet (keyless) is an example). You still have to finish the backup and confirm how recovery works.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WAGMI</td>\n<td>A community slogan, \"we're all gonna make it\". It is not a promise of a return.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. DYOR means you research and check it yourself. Someone else's opinion cannot be treated as the conclusion.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. DYOR is not 'if someone is bullish, you can buy,' and it is not acting because you see FOMO. It means you check it yourself.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>What is DYOR saying?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. If someone else is bullish, you can treat that as the conclusion</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Research and check it yourself, and do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If you see FOMO, you should buy immediately</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Serious terms are community shorthand, and you need the meaning before you can tell a slogan from a fact. The table below lists common words in their existing wording. Knowing the meaning is so you can understand what other people are saying. It does not turn a slogan into a fact. Term Plain explanation DYOR Do your own research and check it yourself. Do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion. FOMO Fear of missing out, which pushes you to jump in on impulse. FUD Fear, uncertainty, and doubt. The word cannot be used to dismiss every criticism. HODL Community slang for holding for a long time. ATH / ATL All-time high / all-time low. Pay attention to the range of the data. Bull / Bear Bullish / bearish. Also used for a bull market / a bear market. Whale A participant with a large holding or a large trade. Degen Someone aggressive about high-risk plays. Often said with self-mockery. Rug Pull The project side abandons participants by pulling funds or by some other exit. Honeypot A trap that looks buyable but may block selling or transferring. Airdrop Assets allocated to users or addresses that qualify. Snapshot A record of state at one moment in time. Vesting Assets released gradually over time or under conditions. TGE Token generation event. Whether it circulates immediately depends on the rules. Whitepaper The project's write-up of its design and plan. It is not a promise that any of it will happen. Tokenomics The token's economics: issuance, allocation, use, and incentives. TVL Total value locked. You need the scope and the definition behind the number. FDV Fully diluted valuation. You need the supply definition behind it. Gas The usual name for the network resources or the fee a transaction needs. TxID The transaction id / hash, used to look the transaction up. Approve An approval that lets a named party use an asset under the rules. Revoke Cancel that approval. It cannot recover assets already lost. Slippage The difference between the price you expected and the price you got. LP Liquidity provider. Also often used for the liquidity position itself. MEV Maximal extractable value: opportunities from ordering, including, or excluding transactions. CEX / DEX Centralized exchange / decentralized trading protocol or venue. KYC The identity-verification process. P2P A user-to-user trade. In crypto it often means buying and selling with fiat. Long / Short Betting on a rise / betting on a fall. Funding Rate The funding rate on a perpetual contract. Liquidation Forced close-out. The exact meaning depends on the setting. GM / GN Community greetings: good morning / good night. 庄家 / 市场操纵 (market maker in the slang sense / manipulation) Casually called 狗庄. A person or group holding enough of a coin to move its price or its volume in an obvious way, for example by concentrated buying and selling, wash trading, or pumping and then distributing. Small-cap, illiquid coins are easier to push around. Meme coin A token built around a culture, a community, and how it spreads. The small, high-risk ones are often called 土狗. They can surge, and they can go to zero or become impossible to sell. Swap Exchange one asset for another. Watch price impact, slippage, and the network fee. Private Key The secret that authorizes actions on an account. Like a seed phrase, never give it to anyone. MPC wallet A multi-party computation wallet. The key is split into shares kept apart. Creating one usually does not mean writing down a traditional seed phrase (Binance Wallet (keyless) is an example). You still have to finish the backup and confirm how recovery works. WAGMI A community slogan, \"we're all gonna make it\". It is not a promise of a return. Think it through What is DYOR saying? A. If someone else is bullish, you can treat that as the conclusion B. Research and check it yourself, and do not treat someone else's opinion as the conclusion C. If you see FOMO, you should buy immediately"}, {"title": "Community jokes", "html": "<p><strong>Community jokes are teasing, and they are not trading advice.</strong></p>\n<p>The lines below are teasing.<br />\nThey are not a judgment of everyone's real intent.<br />\nThey are also not trading advice.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>The joke, in plain words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>长期看好 (long-term bullish)</td>\n<td>Don't ask me about the short term.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>健康回调 (healthy pullback)</td>\n<td>It dropped. I just want a nicer way to say that.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>钻石手 (diamond hands)</td>\n<td>You can hold. You also may not be able to sell for now.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>纸手 (paper hands)</td>\n<td>Sold, and it rose. Bought, and panicked.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>交学费 (paying tuition)</td>\n<td>The money is smaller. Hopefully the experience is larger.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>格局打开 (open up your mindset)</td>\n<td>Hold on. Don't sell yet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>价值投资 (value investing)</td>\n<td>This was supposed to be a short-term trade. Now it is long-term.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>最后一跌 (the last drop)</td>\n<td>That is what was said last time too.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>抄底成功 (caught the bottom)</td>\n<td>So far, all that succeeded is the buy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>回本就走 (I'll leave when I'm back to even)</td>\n<td>The goal just changed from getting rich to getting back to even.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>社区很强 (the community is strong)</td>\n<td>The group chat is certainly lively. The product still needs another look.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>财富自由 (financial freedom)</td>\n<td>For now, the freedom is not looking at the account.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>梭哈 (all-in)</td>\n<td>Risk control is temporarily offline.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>上车 / 下车 (get on / get off)</td>\n<td>Buy / sell. Don't chase just because of the metaphor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>报单 / 喊单 (calling a trade)</td>\n<td>Someone tells you what to buy. If it rises, it was their eye. If it loses, it is yours.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>接盘 (catching the bag)</td>\n<td>Buying what someone else is selling. Often a joke about buying the top.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. In this comparison, a healthy pullback is a nicer way of saying the price dropped. It is not proof that the price will rise, and it is not a price guarantee.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. This is teasing. It does not prove the price will rise, and it is not an official guarantee.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>In this comparison, which meaning is closer to \"healthy pullback\"?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. It already proves the price will rise afterward</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. It dropped, but the wording makes it sound nicer</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. An official price guarantee</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Community jokes are teasing, and they are not trading advice. The lines below are teasing. They are not a judgment of everyone's real intent. They are also not trading advice. Phrase The joke, in plain words 长期看好 (long-term bullish) Don't ask me about the short term. 健康回调 (healthy pullback) It dropped. I just want a nicer way to say that. 钻石手 (diamond hands) You can hold. You also may not be able to sell for now. 纸手 (paper hands) Sold, and it rose. Bought, and panicked. 交学费 (paying tuition) The money is smaller. Hopefully the experience is larger. 格局打开 (open up your mindset) Hold on. Don't sell yet. 价值投资 (value investing) This was supposed to be a short-term trade. Now it is long-term. 最后一跌 (the last drop) That is what was said last time too. 抄底成功 (caught the bottom) So far, all that succeeded is the buy. 回本就走 (I'll leave when I'm back to even) The goal just changed from getting rich to getting back to even. 社区很强 (the community is strong) The group chat is certainly lively. The product still needs another look. 财富自由 (financial freedom) For now, the freedom is not looking at the account. 梭哈 (all-in) Risk control is temporarily offline. 上车 / 下车 (get on / get off) Buy / sell. Don't chase just because of the metaphor. 报单 / 喊单 (calling a trade) Someone tells you what to buy. If it rises, it was their eye. If it loses, it is yours. 接盘 (catching the bag) Buying what someone else is selling. Often a joke about buying the top. Think it through In this comparison, which meaning is closer to \"healthy pullback\"? A. It already proves the price will rise afterward B. It dropped, but the wording makes it sound nicer C. An official price guarantee"}]}, {"id": "14", "title": "Tool guide", "emoji": "🧰", "intro": "Prices, trades, and on-chain data", "tags": ["Guide"], "sections": [{"title": "Prices and basic facts", "html": "<p><strong>For price, market cap, and on-chain data, use these sites.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>CoinGecko</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.coingecko.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Prices, market cap, assets, and market data. Being listed does not mean the project is safe. Check the data.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.coingecko.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.coingecko.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.coingecko.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.coingecko.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>CoinMarketCap</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://coinmarketcap.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Market data, supply, and trading venues. Supply definitions can differ across sites.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>coinmarketcap.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://coinmarketcap.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://coinmarketcap.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, coinmarketcap.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>DefiLlama</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://defillama.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>DeFi, TVL, exchange reserves, and related data. Before you compare, check the definition, the time, and whether anything is counted twice.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>defillama.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://defillama.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://defillama.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, defillama.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>TradingView</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingview.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Charts and technical indicators. Check the exchange, the pair, and the timeframe. A community opinion is not a fact.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.tradingview.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.tradingview.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingview.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.tradingview.com)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "For price, market cap, and on-chain data, use these sites. CoinGecko ⏳ Not checked yet Prices, market cap, assets, and market data. Being listed does not mean the project is safe. Check the data. Domain: www.coingecko.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.coingecko.com) CoinMarketCap ⏳ Not checked yet Market data, supply, and trading venues. Supply definitions can differ across sites. Domain: coinmarketcap.com Open site ↗ (new window, coinmarketcap.com) DefiLlama ⏳ Not checked yet DeFi, TVL, exchange reserves, and related data. Before you compare, check the definition, the time, and whether anything is counted twice. Domain: defillama.com Open site ↗ (new window, defillama.com) TradingView ⏳ Not checked yet Charts and technical indicators. Check the exchange, the pair, and the timeframe. A community opinion is not a fact. Domain: www.tradingview.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.tradingview.com)"}, {"title": "Block explorers, one per chain", "html": "<p><strong>To look up a transfer or an address, use the explorer for that chain.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Etherscan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://etherscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Ethereum transactions, addresses, and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>etherscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://etherscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://etherscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, etherscan.io)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>BscScan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://bscscan.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>BNB Smart Chain transactions and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>bscscan.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://bscscan.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://bscscan.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, bscscan.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Solscan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://solscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Solana transactions and accounts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>solscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://solscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://solscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, solscan.io)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>mempool.space</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://mempool.space/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Bitcoin transactions, blocks, and fees.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>mempool.space</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://mempool.space/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://mempool.space/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, mempool.space)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>TRONSCAN</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://tronscan.org/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>TRON transactions, accounts, and resources.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>tronscan.org</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://tronscan.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://tronscan.org/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, tronscan.org)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Arbiscan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://arbiscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Arbitrum One transactions and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>arbiscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://arbiscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://arbiscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, arbiscan.io)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>BaseScan</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://basescan.org/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Base network transactions and contracts.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>basescan.org</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://basescan.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://basescan.org/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, basescan.org)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Robinhood Chain</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://robin.etherscan.io/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>The Etherscan-style explorer for Robinhood Chain mainnet.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>robin.etherscan.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://robin.etherscan.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://robin.etherscan.io/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, robin.etherscan.io)</span></a></div>\n</div><p>Before you use them: the explorer and the network have to match. Verified contract code is not a security audit. A label is not a substitute for checking identity.</p>", "text": "To look up a transfer or an address, use the explorer for that chain. Etherscan ⏳ Not checked yet Ethereum transactions, addresses, and contracts. Domain: etherscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, etherscan.io) BscScan ⏳ Not checked yet BNB Smart Chain transactions and contracts. Domain: bscscan.com Open site ↗ (new window, bscscan.com) Solscan ⏳ Not checked yet Solana transactions and accounts. Domain: solscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, solscan.io) mempool.space ⏳ Not checked yet Bitcoin transactions, blocks, and fees. Domain: mempool.space Open site ↗ (new window, mempool.space) TRONSCAN ⏳ Not checked yet TRON transactions, accounts, and resources. Domain: tronscan.org Open site ↗ (new window, tronscan.org) Arbiscan ⏳ Not checked yet Arbitrum One transactions and contracts. Domain: arbiscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, arbiscan.io) BaseScan ⏳ Not checked yet Base network transactions and contracts. Domain: basescan.org Open site ↗ (new window, basescan.org) Robinhood Chain ⏳ Not checked yet The Etherscan-style explorer for Robinhood Chain mainnet. Domain: robin.etherscan.io Open site ↗ (new window, robin.etherscan.io) Before you use them: the explorer and the network have to match. Verified contract code is not a security audit. A label is not a substitute for checking identity."}, {"title": "On-chain pairs", "html": "<p><strong>For new on-chain coins and pools, use this.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>DEX Screener</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://dexscreener.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>On-chain pairs, prices, and pool activity. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token can be bought and sold safely.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>dexscreener.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://dexscreener.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://dexscreener.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, dexscreener.com)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "For new on-chain coins and pools, use this. DEX Screener ⏳ Not checked yet On-chain pairs, prices, and pool activity. Being listed in the tool does not mean the token can be bought and sold safely. Domain: dexscreener.com Open site ↗ (new window, dexscreener.com)"}, {"title": "News", "html": "<p><strong>Read the news to follow the industry. For anything important, the official announcement wins.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>PANews</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.panewslab.com/zh\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Chinese-language blockchain and crypto-market news and briefs.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.panewslab.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.panewslab.com/zh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.panewslab.com/zh\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.panewslab.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Odaily</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.odaily.news/zh-CN\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Chinese-language blockchain and crypto news, briefs, and longer pieces.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.odaily.news</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.odaily.news/zh-CN\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.odaily.news/zh-CN\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.odaily.news)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>BlockBeats</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.theblockbeats.info/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Chinese-language crypto-industry briefs and longer reporting.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.theblockbeats.info</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.theblockbeats.info/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.theblockbeats.info/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.theblockbeats.info)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>TradingKey</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingkey.com/zh-hans/news/cryptocurrencies\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Crypto-related news, in Chinese.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.tradingkey.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.tradingkey.com/zh-hans/news/cryptocurrencies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.tradingkey.com/zh-hans/news/cryptocurrencies\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.tradingkey.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>CoinDesk</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.coindesk.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Crypto news and market coverage, in English.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.coindesk.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.coindesk.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.coindesk.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.coindesk.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>The Block</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://www.theblock.co/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Crypto-industry news, data, and research, in English.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>www.theblock.co</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://www.theblock.co/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://www.theblock.co/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, www.theblock.co)</span></a></div>\n</div><p>Before you use them: news and opinion are not investment advice, and outlets also run sponsored content. For anything important, the project's or the institution's own announcement is the source that counts.</p>", "text": "Read the news to follow the industry. For anything important, the official announcement wins. PANews ⏳ Not checked yet Chinese-language blockchain and crypto-market news and briefs. Domain: www.panewslab.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.panewslab.com) Odaily ⏳ Not checked yet Chinese-language blockchain and crypto news, briefs, and longer pieces. Domain: www.odaily.news Open site ↗ (new window, www.odaily.news) BlockBeats ⏳ Not checked yet Chinese-language crypto-industry briefs and longer reporting. Domain: www.theblockbeats.info Open site ↗ (new window, www.theblockbeats.info) TradingKey ⏳ Not checked yet Crypto-related news, in Chinese. Domain: www.tradingkey.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.tradingkey.com) CoinDesk ⏳ Not checked yet Crypto news and market coverage, in English. Domain: www.coindesk.com Open site ↗ (new window, www.coindesk.com) The Block ⏳ Not checked yet Crypto-industry news, data, and research, in English. Domain: www.theblock.co Open site ↗ (new window, www.theblock.co) Before you use them: news and opinion are not investment advice, and outlets also run sponsored content. For anything important, the project's or the institution's own announcement is the source that counts."}, {"title": "Checking and revoking approvals", "html": "<p><strong>Check approvals now and then, and revoke the ones you no longer use. These tools can help you check and revoke approvals, but they cannot guarantee the assets will not be stolen.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Revoke.cash</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://revoke.cash/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Check approvals and revoke them. It can inspect some asset approvals on supported networks, and revoke them when you choose. Network and permission coverage is limited. Check the domain and the signature before you use it. If a private key has leaked, revoking approvals is not enough on its own.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>revoke.cash</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://revoke.cash/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://revoke.cash/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, revoke.cash)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>GoPlus</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Check approvals and revoke them, and it also offers a browser extension and similar risk prompts. Coverage is limited. A risk prompt is not a substitute for checking it yourself.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>gopluslabs.io</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://gopluslabs.io/en\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, gopluslabs.io)</span></a></div></div>", "text": "Check approvals now and then, and revoke the ones you no longer use. These tools can help you check and revoke approvals, but they cannot guarantee the assets will not be stolen. Revoke.cash ⏳ Not checked yet Check approvals and revoke them. It can inspect some asset approvals on supported networks, and revoke them when you choose. Network and permission coverage is limited. Check the domain and the signature before you use it. If a private key has leaked, revoking approvals is not enough on its own. Domain: revoke.cash Open site ↗ (new window, revoke.cash) GoPlus ⏳ Not checked yet Check approvals and revoke them, and it also offers a browser extension and similar risk prompts. Coverage is limited. A risk prompt is not a substitute for checking it yourself. Domain: gopluslabs.io Open site ↗ (new window, gopluslabs.io)"}, {"title": "Learning material", "html": "<p><strong>To go deeper, read these official sources.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tool-grid\"><div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Binance Academy</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://academy.binance.com/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>A beginner crypto encyclopedia, friendly to Chinese readers.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>academy.binance.com</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://academy.binance.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://academy.binance.com/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, academy.binance.com)</span></a></div>\n<div class=\"tool-card\"><div class=\"tool-head\"><strong>Ethereum security</strong><span class=\"verify\" data-url=\"https://ethereum.org/security/\">⏳ Not checked yet</span></div><p>Ethereum&#x27;s own safety and anti-scam guide.</p><p class=\"domain\">Domain: <b>ethereum.org</b></p><a class=\"btn btn-sm ext\" href=\"https://ethereum.org/security/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" data-url=\"https://ethereum.org/security/\">Open site ↗ <span class=\"sr\">(new window, ethereum.org)</span></a></div>\n</div><p>Before you use them: official material is good for checking concepts and product rules. A platform's own commercial description still needs to be read as marketing.</p>", "text": "To go deeper, read these official sources. Binance Academy ⏳ Not checked yet A beginner crypto encyclopedia, friendly to Chinese readers. Domain: academy.binance.com Open site ↗ (new window, academy.binance.com) Ethereum security ⏳ Not checked yet Ethereum's own safety and anti-scam guide. Domain: ethereum.org Open site ↗ (new window, ethereum.org) Before you use them: official material is good for checking concepts and product rules. A platform's own commercial description still needs to be read as marketing."}, {"title": "Unlocks and official information", "html": "<p><strong>For an unlock schedule, read the project's own docs first, then compare third-party data.</strong></p>\n<p>Start with the project's official documents for allocation, lockups, and unlock rules, then compare them with third-party data. Plans can change. A label that says \"estimated\" is not the same thing as tokens actually released on-chain.</p>\n<p>Every tool answers only part of the question. After you find a number, you still need to know what it is, how it was counted, and what is missing.</p>", "text": "For an unlock schedule, read the project's own docs first, then compare third-party data. Start with the project's official documents for allocation, lockups, and unlock rules, then compare them with third-party data. Plans can change. A label that says \"estimated\" is not the same thing as tokens actually released on-chain. Every tool answers only part of the question. After you find a number, you still need to know what it is, how it was counted, and what is missing."}]}, {"id": "15", "title": "Something went wrong", "emoji": "🆘", "intro": "Deposits, withdrawals, transfers, and suspected theft", "tags": ["Troubleshooting"], "sections": [{"title": "A deposit has not arrived", "html": "<p>Success on-chain is not the same as a credit at the exchange: check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations, then use the official channel, and do not send again or hand over a seed phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>The money has been sent, and the exchange is not showing a balance.</strong><br />\nDo not rush to send it again.</p>\n<p>First, open the withdrawal record and see whether the platform has already sent the transaction, and whether there is a TxID.<br />\nSecond, look it up in the block explorer for that network.<br />\nIf it is not on-chain yet, go to the platform that sent it.<br />\nIf it is on-chain and still waiting for confirmations, look at the confirmation status first.<br />\nIf it succeeded on-chain but has not been credited, check the receiving address, the Memo / Tag, the coin, the network, the minimum deposit, and the number of confirmations the platform requires.<br />\nIf the details are right and it still has not arrived, take the TxID and the order record to the receiving platform's official support.<br />\nA success on-chain and a credit on the platform are two different things. Arrival is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A success on-chain is not the same as a credit on the platform. Check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations first, then submit the TxID through the official channel.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Sending again can make the loss larger. Giving a seed phrase to support hands over control of the wallet.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the TxID already shows success, but the exchange balance is still 0. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send another transfer to the same address</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations the platform requires</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Send the seed phrase to support so they can check the balance</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Success on-chain is not the same as a credit at the exchange: check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations, then use the official channel, and do not send again or hand over a seed phrase. The money has been sent, and the exchange is not showing a balance. Do not rush to send it again. First, open the withdrawal record and see whether the platform has already sent the transaction, and whether there is a TxID. Second, look it up in the block explorer for that network. If it is not on-chain yet, go to the platform that sent it. If it is on-chain and still waiting for confirmations, look at the confirmation status first. If it succeeded on-chain but has not been credited, check the receiving address, the Memo / Tag, the coin, the network, the minimum deposit, and the number of confirmations the platform requires. If the details are right and it still has not arrived, take the TxID and the order record to the receiving platform's official support. A success on-chain and a credit on the platform are two different things. Arrival is not guaranteed. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the TxID already shows success, but the exchange balance is still 0. What should you do? A. Send another transfer to the same address B. Check the network, the Memo, and the confirmations the platform requires C. Send the seed phrase to support so they can check the balance"}, {"title": "A withdrawal stays on \"processing\"", "html": "<p>While a withdrawal stays on processing, read the order and look for a TxID; if there is none, use the official entrance, and do not pay a speed-up fee to a private message.</p>\n<p><strong>The withdrawal page keeps spinning. Look at the order status first, then look for a TxID.</strong></p>\n<p>No TxID usually means you cannot yet confirm that the transaction has been broadcast to the chain.<br />\nIt may still be in review, in a queue, or being processed.<br />\nIn that case, read the platform notice and the order notes, and ask for help through the official entrance.<br />\nIf there is a TxID, check the confirmation status in the explorer for that network.<br />\nDo not send a so-called fee to someone who messages you and says they can speed the withdrawal up.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. When there is no TxID, read the platform order and notices first, then use the official entrance. Do not pay a speed-up fee to a private message.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Without a TxID you cannot yet confirm it is on-chain. Guessing a hash, or paying a speed-up fee, does not replace the official order.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the order says processing, and there is no TxID. Which next step is the steadier one?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Guess a hash in a block explorer and look that up</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Read the platform order and notices, and contact official support</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Pay a speed-up fee to the support account that messaged you</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "While a withdrawal stays on processing, read the order and look for a TxID; if there is none, use the official entrance, and do not pay a speed-up fee to a private message. The withdrawal page keeps spinning. Look at the order status first, then look for a TxID. No TxID usually means you cannot yet confirm that the transaction has been broadcast to the chain. It may still be in review, in a queue, or being processed. In that case, read the platform notice and the order notes, and ask for help through the official entrance. If there is a TxID, check the confirmation status in the explorer for that network. Do not send a so-called fee to someone who messages you and says they can speed the withdrawal up. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the order says processing, and there is no TxID. Which next step is the steadier one? A. Guess a hash in a block explorer and look that up B. Read the platform order and notices, and contact official support C. Pay a speed-up fee to the support account that messaged you"}, {"title": "Sent to the wrong address or the wrong network", "html": "<p>If you sent to the wrong address or the wrong network, give the TxID to the receiver's official channel and ask whether recovery is possible; recovery is not guaranteed, and do not send again or give away a seed phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>Sending to the wrong address and sending on the wrong network are not the same problem.</strong></p>\n<p>Stop sending anything further.<br />\nSave the TxID, the receiving address, the coin, the network, and the time.<br />\nIf it went to an address you do not recognize, an on-chain transaction generally cannot be undone.<br />\nWhether it can be recovered depends on whether the other side cooperates.<br />\nIf it went to a network the exchange does not support, contact the receiving platform and ask whether a recovery service exists.<br />\nIf it is an address you control, first confirm that the wallet and the key support that network.<br />\nDo not casually import the private key into a tool you do not know.<br />\nIn every case, recovery is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. When the network is not supported, give the TxID to the receiving platform's official channel and ask whether recovery is possible. Recovery is not guaranteed.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Sending again does not fix the previous transfer. Do not give a seed phrase to a site you do not know.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the TxID shows success, but the receiving exchange does not support the network you chose. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send again, on a cheaper network</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Give the official channel the TxID and ask whether a recovery service exists</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Type the seed phrase into an unfamiliar recovery site</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If you sent to the wrong address or the wrong network, give the TxID to the receiver's official channel and ask whether recovery is possible; recovery is not guaranteed, and do not send again or give away a seed phrase. Sending to the wrong address and sending on the wrong network are not the same problem. Stop sending anything further. Save the TxID, the receiving address, the coin, the network, and the time. If it went to an address you do not recognize, an on-chain transaction generally cannot be undone. Whether it can be recovered depends on whether the other side cooperates. If it went to a network the exchange does not support, contact the receiving platform and ask whether a recovery service exists. If it is an address you control, first confirm that the wallet and the key support that network. Do not casually import the private key into a tool you do not know. In every case, recovery is not guaranteed. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the TxID shows success, but the receiving exchange does not support the network you chose. What should you do? A. Send again, on a cheaper network B. Give the official channel the TxID and ask whether a recovery service exists C. Type the seed phrase into an unfamiliar recovery site"}, {"title": "You forgot the Memo / Tag", "html": "<p>If the Memo or Tag was left blank, keep the TxID and the order and use the official process; recovery follows the platform's rules and is not guaranteed.</p>\n<p><strong>Some platforms share one deposit address among many users. The Memo or Tag is what says which account the money belongs to.</strong></p>\n<p>The address and the network can both be right, and the chain can show success, and a missing Memo can still mean the money reached the platform's address without being credited to your account.<br />\nSave the TxID, the coin, the amount, the network, and the deposit order first.<br />\nThen apply through the platform's official process for a missing Memo / Tag.<br />\nWhether recovery is offered, how long it takes, and whether it costs money all depend on the platform's rules. It is not guaranteed.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A missing Memo means you keep the TxID and the order, and you use the platform's official process. Whether it can be recovered depends on the platform's rules, and it is not guaranteed.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Another deposit does not fill in the Memo on the previous one. You also cannot edit the record on a block explorer.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the deposit address is correct, the Memo was left blank, and the chain already shows success. What next?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Deposit again immediately, and put the Memo on the next transfer</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Gather the TxID and the order, and submit them through the official missing-Memo process</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Edit the record on the block explorer</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If the Memo or Tag was left blank, keep the TxID and the order and use the official process; recovery follows the platform's rules and is not guaranteed. Some platforms share one deposit address among many users. The Memo or Tag is what says which account the money belongs to. The address and the network can both be right, and the chain can show success, and a missing Memo can still mean the money reached the platform's address without being credited to your account. Save the TxID, the coin, the amount, the network, and the deposit order first. Then apply through the platform's official process for a missing Memo / Tag. Whether recovery is offered, how long it takes, and whether it costs money all depend on the platform's rules. It is not guaranteed. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the deposit address is correct, the Memo was left blank, and the chain already shows success. What next? A. Deposit again immediately, and put the Memo on the next transfer B. Gather the TxID and the order, and submit them through the official missing-Memo process C. Edit the record on the block explorer"}, {"title": "The wallet does not show the asset", "html": "<p>When the wallet screen shows no coins, check the account, the network, and whether this token is displayed, and do not upload a seed phrase.</p>\n<p><strong>A coin suddenly missing from the wallet screen does not mean the coin is definitely gone.</strong></p>\n<p>First check whether you switched account or network.<br />\nThen use a block explorer you trust and look at that address's balance and transfers on the matching network.<br />\nIf the balance is still on-chain, the wallet may simply not be displaying that token.<br />\nCheck the contract address from a trusted source, then add it so the wallet can show it.<br />\nIf the on-chain record shows the asset already left, treat it as a suspicious transaction.<br />\nDo not believe anyone who says you must enter a seed phrase to sync the balance.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"a\" data-ok=\"Correct. When the balance is still on-chain, check the account, the network, and whether the token is displayed. A seed phrase cannot be used to sync a balance.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A screen showing 0 does not mean the chain is empty. Do not upload a seed phrase, and do not decide it was stolen from the screen alone.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the block explorer shows 100 USDT at this address, and the wallet app shows 0. Which step is the more likely first one?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Check the account, the network, and whether this token is being displayed</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Upload the seed phrase so a site can sync the balance</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Decide immediately that the asset was stolen</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When the wallet screen shows no coins, check the account, the network, and whether this token is displayed, and do not upload a seed phrase. A coin suddenly missing from the wallet screen does not mean the coin is definitely gone. First check whether you switched account or network. Then use a block explorer you trust and look at that address's balance and transfers on the matching network. If the balance is still on-chain, the wallet may simply not be displaying that token. Check the contract address from a trusted source, then add it so the wallet can show it. If the on-chain record shows the asset already left, treat it as a suspicious transaction. Do not believe anyone who says you must enter a seed phrase to sync the balance. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the block explorer shows 100 USDT at this address, and the wallet app shows 0. Which step is the more likely first one? A. Check the account, the network, and whether this token is being displayed B. Upload the seed phrase so a site can sync the balance C. Decide immediately that the asset was stolen"}, {"title": "A transaction failed or is stuck", "html": "<p>When a transaction fails or sticks, first check whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast; a failure may already have spent the network fee, so do not keep tapping blindly.</p>\n<p><strong>An on-chain transaction has not finished. First find out whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast.</strong></p>\n<p>While it is pending, some networks and wallets can speed it up or replace it, but not every chain works the same way.<br />\nA transaction that already failed may still have spent the network fee.<br />\nIf it was never broadcast, check the balance, the network, and the wallet's message.<br />\nDo not tap submit many times in a row.<br />\nRead the transaction record and the official instructions first, then decide whether to try again.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A failed transaction may already have spent the network fee. Read the reason and the official instructions first, and do not keep tapping.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A failure does not mean the network fee comes back. Submitting again and again does not guarantee the next one succeeds.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the explorer shows this transaction failed, and the network fee was already charged. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. A failure always refunds the network fee</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Look up why it failed, then decide whether to try again</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Keep submitting until one succeeds</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When a transaction fails or sticks, first check whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast; a failure may already have spent the network fee, so do not keep tapping blindly. An on-chain transaction has not finished. First find out whether it is pending, failed, or never broadcast. While it is pending, some networks and wallets can speed it up or replace it, but not every chain works the same way. A transaction that already failed may still have spent the network fee. If it was never broadcast, check the balance, the network, and the wallet's message. Do not tap submit many times in a row. Read the transaction record and the official instructions first, then decide whether to try again. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the explorer shows this transaction failed, and the network fee was already charged. What should you do? A. A failure always refunds the network fee B. Look up why it failed, then decide whether to try again C. Keep submitting until one succeeds"}, {"title": "You suspect a bad approval or a bad signature", "html": "<p>If you suspect a bad signature or approval, stop first; disconnecting is not the same as revoking, so check and revoke it, then judge the risk that remains.</p>\n<p><strong>If you just signed or approved something on a site you do not know, stop. Do not click another popup on that site.</strong></p>\n<p>Save the site address, the transaction hash, or the contents of the signature.<br />\nCheck again from a device or an environment you trust.<br />\nIf it was a token approval, you can check it with a tool you trust and revoke it if you need to.<br />\nIf some other signature permission or control of the account is involved, revoking an ordinary approval may not be enough.<br />\nYou may then need to move the remaining assets to a new wallet that is safe.<br />\nDo not treat \"I disconnected the site\" as \"the risk is gone\".</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Disconnecting does not cancel an approval you already gave. Check it and revoke it, and decide whether the remaining assets should be moved.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Disconnecting is not the same as revoking. Do not go back to that site and sign again.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: you gave an unknown contract an unlimited allowance. Is disconnecting the site enough?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Disconnecting is the same as revoking the approval</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Check and revoke the approval, then judge the risk that remains</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Sign once more, to verify that the earlier one was fine</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If you suspect a bad signature or approval, stop first; disconnecting is not the same as revoking, so check and revoke it, then judge the risk that remains. If you just signed or approved something on a site you do not know, stop. Do not click another popup on that site. Save the site address, the transaction hash, or the contents of the signature. Check again from a device or an environment you trust. If it was a token approval, you can check it with a tool you trust and revoke it if you need to. If some other signature permission or control of the account is involved, revoking an ordinary approval may not be enough. You may then need to move the remaining assets to a new wallet that is safe. Do not treat \"I disconnected the site\" as \"the risk is gone\". Think it through Fictional teaching example: you gave an unknown contract an unlimited allowance. Is disconnecting the site enough? A. Yes. Disconnecting is the same as revoking the approval B. No. Check and revoke the approval, then judge the risk that remains C. Sign once more, to verify that the earlier one was fine"}, {"title": "The seed phrase or private key leaked", "html": "<p>Once a seed phrase or private key has leaked, changing a password or reinstalling does not retire the old key, so create a new wallet before you judge how to move what remains.</p>\n<p><strong>If a seed phrase or private key has been given to someone else, uploaded to a web page, or exposed in an environment you do not trust, that wallet is no longer safe.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"keep-lines\">First, on a device you trust, create a wallet with a brand-new key.<br>\nThen judge how to move any remaining assets there safely.<br>\nChanging only the app password, uninstalling the wallet, or revoking an approval<br>\ncannot make a private key that has already leaked stop working.<br>\nIf you suspect a program is automatically sweeping assets, and you add network fee to the old wallet,<br>\nthat fee may be swept away immediately too.<br>\nIn that situation, seek trusted professional help first.</p>\n\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A leaked private key does not stop working because you changed a password or reinstalled. Use a new key first, then consider how to move what remains.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Changing a password and uninstalling cannot make the old private key stop working. Do not keep treating that address as a safe wallet.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the seed phrase was already sent to fake support. Can the old address still be used as a safe wallet?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. Change the login password and keep using it</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. Create a brand-new wallet, then judge how to move the remaining assets safely</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. After you uninstall and reinstall, the old private key stops working</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Once a seed phrase or private key has leaked, changing a password or reinstalling does not retire the old key, so create a new wallet before you judge how to move what remains. If a seed phrase or private key has been given to someone else, uploaded to a web page, or exposed in an environment you do not trust, that wallet is no longer safe. First, on a device you trust, create a wallet with a brand-new key. Then judge how to move any remaining assets there safely. Changing only the app password, uninstalling the wallet, or revoking an approval cannot make a private key that has already leaked stop working. If you suspect a program is automatically sweeping assets, and you add network fee to the old wallet, that fee may be swept away immediately too. In that situation, seek trusted professional help first. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the seed phrase was already sent to fake support. Can the old address still be used as a safe wallet? A. Yes. Change the login password and keep using it B. No. Create a brand-new wallet, then judge how to move the remaining assets safely C. After you uninstall and reinstall, the old private key stops working"}, {"title": "The exchange account may have been taken over", "html": "<p>If the exchange account may have been taken over, restrict it from the official entrance first, then check the email, the API, and the withdrawal settings, and do not give a verification code to a private message.</p>\n<p><strong>If you see an unfamiliar login, a withdrawal record, or an API action, restrict the account through the exchange's official entrance first.</strong></p>\n<p>Do not click a suspicious link in a text or an email.<br />\nProtect the linked email at the same time.<br />\nCheck login devices, sessions, API keys, and the withdrawal allowlist.<br />\nUpdate the password and the verification method through the official process.<br />\nIf funds are already gone, save the notices, the orders, the addresses, and the times.<br />\nThen report it through a proper channel where you live.<br />\nThe order of work is to stop further loss first.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Restrict the account from the official entrance first, protect the email, then check the API, the devices, and the withdrawal settings.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Changing only the trading password does not stop an unknown API. Do not give a verification code or login details to someone in a private message.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: the email warns of a login from somewhere else, and the account also shows an API you never created. What is the first step?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Change only the trading password, and leave the rest</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Restrict the account from the official entrance, and check the email and the API</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Reply to the stranger who sent the alert, and give them the verification code</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If the exchange account may have been taken over, restrict it from the official entrance first, then check the email, the API, and the withdrawal settings, and do not give a verification code to a private message. If you see an unfamiliar login, a withdrawal record, or an API action, restrict the account through the exchange's official entrance first. Do not click a suspicious link in a text or an email. Protect the linked email at the same time. Check login devices, sessions, API keys, and the withdrawal allowlist. Update the password and the verification method through the official process. If funds are already gone, save the notices, the orders, the addresses, and the times. Then report it through a proper channel where you live. The order of work is to stop further loss first. Think it through Fictional teaching example: the email warns of a login from somewhere else, and the account also shows an API you never created. What is the first step? A. Change only the trading password, and leave the rest B. Restrict the account from the official entrance, and check the email and the API C. Reply to the stranger who sent the alert, and give them the verification code"}, {"title": "A second scam, after the first", "html": "<p>If someone asks you to pay first, hand over a seed phrase, or give remote control to recover funds, that is not a reliable method, so use only the official channel.</p>\n<p><strong>Right after a funds problem, you are easiest for a \"recovery expert\", \"technical support\", or a \"paid unfreeze\" pitch to find.</strong></p>\n<p>Be very wary of a private message that promises a one-hundred-percent recovery, asks for a deposit first, asks for a seed phrase, or asks to control your device remotely.<br />\nCheck the official channel, and keep the evidence.<br />\nReal official support should not ask you to hand over a seed phrase either.<br />\nDo not hand the remaining assets to a stranger just because you want the money back quickly.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Paying first, asking for a seed phrase, or asking for remote control is not a reliable way to recover funds. Use only the official channel.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not pay first, and do not give a private key to someone in a private message. Wanting it back quickly is not a reason to hand over what remains.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Fictional teaching example: someone messages you and says that paying 50 USDT first will recover the lost coins. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Pay first, so you do not miss the recovery window</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Refuse to pay, and check only through the official channel</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Send them the private key, to prove the address is yours</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "If someone asks you to pay first, hand over a seed phrase, or give remote control to recover funds, that is not a reliable method, so use only the official channel. Right after a funds problem, you are easiest for a \"recovery expert\", \"technical support\", or a \"paid unfreeze\" pitch to find. Be very wary of a private message that promises a one-hundred-percent recovery, asks for a deposit first, asks for a seed phrase, or asks to control your device remotely. Check the official channel, and keep the evidence. Real official support should not ask you to hand over a seed phrase either. Do not hand the remaining assets to a stranger just because you want the money back quickly. Think it through Fictional teaching example: someone messages you and says that paying 50 USDT first will recover the lost coins. What should you do? A. Pay first, so you do not miss the recovery window B. Refuse to pay, and check only through the official channel C. Send them the private key, to prove the address is yours"}, {"title": "What to have ready when you ask for help", "html": "<p>When you ask for help, have the TxID, the order number, and what happened ready; use the official channel, and never provide a seed phrase or a private key.</p>\n<p><strong>Before you contact support, write down a short record of the problem.</strong></p>\n<p>Write the time, the coin and the amount, the sending and receiving networks, the address, the TxID, the platform order number, the error message, and what you have already checked.<br />\nThat cuts down the back and forth.<br />\nBefore a screenshot, cover the password, the verification code, and the ID number.<br />\nA seed phrase and a private key must never be provided.<br />\nIf this happened inside a platform account, use the official ticket.<br />\nDo not post a full set of personal details in a public group.</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. A TxID and an order number can help the official side check. A seed phrase and a private key must not appear in the material you send for help.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. A seed phrase must not be provided, and do not post full details in a public group. A TxID and an order number go through an official ticket.\"><h3>Think it through</h3><p>Support asks for the TxID, the order number, and the seed phrase at the same time. Which choice is right?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send all three into a public group so everyone can look</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. The TxID and the order number can be given through the official process. The seed phrase is never given</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Send only the seed phrase, and do not keep the order number</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "When you ask for help, have the TxID, the order number, and what happened ready; use the official channel, and never provide a seed phrase or a private key. Before you contact support, write down a short record of the problem. Write the time, the coin and the amount, the sending and receiving networks, the address, the TxID, the platform order number, the error message, and what you have already checked. That cuts down the back and forth. Before a screenshot, cover the password, the verification code, and the ID number. A seed phrase and a private key must never be provided. If this happened inside a platform account, use the official ticket. Do not post a full set of personal details in a public group. Think it through Support asks for the TxID, the order number, and the seed phrase at the same time. Which choice is right? A. Send all three into a public group so everyone can look B. The TxID and the order number can be given through the official process. The seed phrase is never given C. Send only the seed phrase, and do not keep the order number"}]}, {"id": "16", "title": "Common questions and practice", "emoji": "✅", "intro": "Check that the key ideas stuck", "tags": ["Review"], "sections": [{"title": "Do I have to buy a coin before I can learn Web3?", "html": "<p><strong>You can learn without buying.</strong></p>\n<p>No. You can read, watch the market, use a paper trade, or use a test network. Learning and investing are two different things.</p>", "text": "You can learn without buying. No. You can read, watch the market, use a paper trade, or use a test network. Learning and investing are two different things."}, {"title": "Is USDT the same as the US dollar?", "html": "<p><strong>USDT is not the dollar. It tries to stay near 1:1 with the dollar.</strong></p>\n<p>No. It is a stablecoin that aims to track the dollar's value. Issuance, redemption, reserves, and risks are not the same as dollar cash or a bank deposit.</p>", "text": "USDT is not the dollar. It tries to stay near 1:1 with the dollar. No. It is a stablecoin that aims to track the dollar's value. Issuance, redemption, reserves, and risks are not the same as dollar cash or a bank deposit."}, {"title": "BTC is too expensive. What if I cannot buy a whole one?", "html": "<p><strong>You can buy 0.001. You do not have to buy a whole coin.</strong></p>\n<p>You can usually buy a fraction. You do not have to buy one whole coin. You still have to meet the platform's minimum order, and you still have to understand the asset's risk.</p>", "text": "You can buy 0.001. You do not have to buy a whole coin. You can usually buy a fraction. You do not have to buy one whole coin. You still have to meet the platform's minimum order, and you still have to understand the asset's risk."}, {"title": "If it is in a cold wallet, can it never be stolen?", "html": "<p><strong>A cold wallet can still be stolen. The usual causes are the seed phrase and signing the wrong thing.</strong></p>\n<p>There is no guarantee. A leaked seed phrase, a device problem, a malicious signature, and a mistaken action can all cause a loss.</p>", "text": "A cold wallet can still be stolen. The usual causes are the seed phrase and signing the wrong thing. There is no guarantee. A leaked seed phrase, a device problem, a malicious signature, and a mistaken action can all cause a loss."}, {"title": "Is an exchange wallet the same as that brand's Web3 wallet?", "html": "<p><strong>Same brand, and the exchange account and the on-chain wallet are still two different things.</strong></p>\n<p>Not necessarily. The same brand does not mean the same custody. Read, for the specific product, who controls the keys, how recovery works, and how withdrawals work.</p>", "text": "Same brand, and the exchange account and the on-chain wallet are still two different things. Not necessarily. The same brand does not mean the same custody. Read, for the specific product, who controls the keys, how recovery works, and how withdrawals work."}, {"title": "Is an airdrop always free?", "html": "<p><strong>An airdrop is not always free. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam.</strong></p>\n<p>Not always. It can cost time, network fees, or even funds, and there may be no reward at the end. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam.</p>", "text": "An airdrop is not always free. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam. Not always. It can cost time, network fees, or even funds, and there may be no reward at the end. A \"claim it free\" page can also be a scam."}, {"title": "If a project was audited and listed, is it safe?", "html": "<p><strong>An audit and a listing are not a safety guarantee.</strong></p>\n<p>They can be part of the research. They do not replace a full judgment of risk, and they do not guarantee a return or that the assets are safe.</p>", "text": "An audit and a listing are not a safety guarantee. They can be part of the research. They do not replace a full judgment of risk, and they do not guarantee a return or that the assets are safe."}, {"title": "If I learn the indicators, can I make money steadily?", "html": "<p><strong>Learning the indicators does not mean steady profits.</strong></p>\n<p>You cannot infer that. Indicators have to be read against the market and against how you execute. Fees, risk management, and emotion also change the result.</p>", "text": "Learning the indicators does not mean steady profits. You cannot infer that. Indicators have to be read against the market and against how you execute. Fees, risk management, and emotion also change the result."}, {"title": "Does crypto have no weekends?", "html": "<p><strong>Crypto trades 24 hours. You do not have to watch it for 24 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>Many crypto markets trade continuously. Bank deposits, some assets, and platform maintenance can still have hours. The market being open does not mean a person has to stare at it.</p>", "text": "Crypto trades 24 hours. You do not have to watch it for 24 hours. Many crypto markets trade continuously. Bank deposits, some assets, and platform maintenance can still have hours. The market being open does not mean a person has to stare at it."}, {"title": "Safety quiz", "html": "<div data-widget=\"quiz\"></div>", "text": "1. \"Official support\" asks for your seed phrase to repair the wallet. Do you give it? Answer: No. Stop the conversation and check the channel. 2. An Ethereum address and a Base address can look the same. Can you pick the network at random? Answer: No. The recipient has to support that network and that asset. 3. If you disconnect a site, are previous token approvals revoked? Answer: Usually not. You have to check them separately. 4. A coin priced at 0.001 dollar is always cheaper than a coin priced at 100 dollars. True? Answer: You cannot judge it that way. You also have to look at supply, valuation, and the asset itself. 5. You set a stop. Is the real loss guaranteed to stay within the amount you planned? Answer: No. Slippage, a price jump, how the order works, and execution problems can all change the result. 6. A stablecoin shows 20% annualized. Does that mean you are guaranteed 20% more after one year? Answer: No. The annualized figure is an estimate under current conditions. It changes with the market. It is not a guaranteed return. Result copy: All correct: \"Basic shield unlocked 🛡️ Keep it up. Still check before you act.\" Any wrong: \"A few spots are worth another look 🧸 Follow the answer back to the chapter.\""}, {"title": "Practice spot P&L calculator", "html": "<div data-widget=\"calculator\"></div>", "text": "Enter the buy price, the sell price, and the cost (how much you spent to buy). A simplified spot example only: one buy and one sell, in the same quote coin. Quantity bought = cost ÷ buy price. Proceeds = quantity bought × sell price. Net profit or loss = proceeds − cost. Simplified return = net profit or loss ÷ cost × 100% (= sell price ÷ buy price − 1). Trading fees, slippage, and similar costs are not included. Invalid input: \"Enter valid numbers. Buy price and cost must be greater than 0. The sell price cannot be negative.\" Result note: \"This is a simplified learning result. Fees are not included. It does not apply to contracts, lending, trades in several fills, or fees in more than one coin. A full account can look different.\""}, {"title": "Checklist before a transfer", "html": "<div data-widget=\"checklist\"></div>", "text": "- I know which asset I am sending. - The recipient explicitly supports this network. - I checked the full address against a source I trust, and I checked the paste or the scan and the final confirmation screen. - I checked the Memo / Tag requirement. - I checked the minimum amount, the fees, and whether anything is paused. - I confirmed I have enough network resources, or a way for someone to pay the fee for me. - The first time I use a path, I send a small test that meets the minimum. Done message: \"Check finished. Check once more on the final confirmation screen. A ticked list cannot guarantee there is no risk.\""}]}, {"id": "17", "title": "Before you transfer: safety basics that are easy to skip", "emoji": "💸", "intro": "The same brand is not the same account. Confirm the platform, the payment details, and the network before you transfer", "tags": ["Must-read"], "sections": [{"title": "The same brand is not the same account", "html": "<p><strong>Platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>See this first: a brand relationship is not shared accounts</h3>\n<div class=\"cosmic-scene compare-scene\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two independent platform accounts under one brand. Do not assume an internal transfer between them\"><div class=\"scene-halo halo-violet\"></div><div class=\"scene-halo halo-cyan\"></div>\n<div class=\"brand-constellation\"><span class=\"star-glyph\">✦</span><span>Same brand · different account systems</span></div>\n<div class=\"account-worlds\"><div class=\"world\"><div class=\"planet planet-violet\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-intl.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance international app icon\"></div><div class=\"world-card\"><b>Binance international</b></div></div>\n<div class=\"world\"><div class=\"planet planet-cyan\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-th.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance TH app icon\"></div><div class=\"world-card\"><b>Binance TH</b></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"scene-divider\"><i></i><span>Do not assume they transfer internally</span><i></i></div>\n<div class=\"scene-bottom\">Same brand is not the same account · the transfer method follows the official rules</div></div>\n<div class=\"caption\">A diagram of the structure. It does not describe how the platform's database is actually built.</div></div>\n\n<p>A recent case: a user wanted to move assets from Binance international to Binance TH (the Thailand version). The transfer went wrong because they assumed the two platforms' accounts were connected.</p>\n<p>The person had not failed to check the receiving UID. They missed a different point: <strong>platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system.</strong></p>\n<p>Take Binance international and Binance TH as the example.</p>\n<p>They both use the Binance brand, but they are different services, and the account systems do not connect directly.</p>\n<p>There is a simple way to judge it:</p>\n<p>If you already registered on platform A, and you try to register the same account system again with the same email, it usually says this email is already registered.</p>\n<p>If the same email can register an account in two apps under the same brand, and you even have to complete KYC again, that is already a clear signal: these two platforms' account systems do not connect directly.</p>\n<p>Because they are two independent accounts, you have to confirm before a transfer whether the platforms support moving funds between them. Do not look only at the brand name.</p>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>Remember:</strong> two independent systems cannot send by UID, unless the official side has explicitly provided a dedicated transfer route.</div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. The same brand and the same email do not mean the two platforms can send to each other directly by UID. Check first whether the official side offers that route.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. The same brand, the same email, and KYC on both still do not mean you can send by UID by default. Check the transfer route the official side supports.\"><h3>Decide</h3><p>I registered on Binance international and on Binance TH. Both accounts use the same email, and both completed KYC. Since both are Binance platforms, can I move funds between the two accounts directly by UID?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes. The same brand means you can transfer</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Do not assume it is supported. Check the official transfer route first</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "Platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system. See this first: a brand relationship is not shared accounts ✦ Same brand · different account systems Binance international Binance TH Do not assume they transfer internally Same brand is not the same account · the transfer method follows the official rules A diagram of the structure. It does not describe how the platform's database is actually built. A recent case: a user wanted to move assets from Binance international to Binance TH (the Thailand version). The transfer went wrong because they assumed the two platforms' accounts were connected. The person had not failed to check the receiving UID. They missed a different point: platforms under the same brand do not mean they use the same account system. Take Binance international and Binance TH as the example. They both use the Binance brand, but they are different services, and the account systems do not connect directly. There is a simple way to judge it: If you already registered on platform A, and you try to register the same account system again with the same email, it usually says this email is already registered. If the same email can register an account in two apps under the same brand, and you even have to complete KYC again, that is already a clear signal: these two platforms' account systems do not connect directly. Because they are two independent accounts, you have to confirm before a transfer whether the platforms support moving funds between them. Do not look only at the brand name. Remember: two independent systems cannot send by UID, unless the official side has explicitly provided a dedicated transfer route. Decide I registered on Binance international and on Binance TH. Both accounts use the same email, and both completed KYC. Since both are Binance platforms, can I move funds between the two accounts directly by UID? A. Yes. The same brand means you can transfer B. Do not assume it is supported. Check the official transfer route first"}, {"title": "The UID is right — why can the transfer still go wrong?", "html": "<p><strong>A UID is only a user number inside one platform, not a receiving identity for the whole industry.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>The same number does not mean the same recipient</h3>\n<div class=\"orbit-scene\" aria-label=\"Two platforms each manage their own UIDs. The same number does not mean the same recipient\"><div class=\"space-glow\"></div><div class=\"orbit-ring ring-a\"></div><div class=\"orbit-ring ring-b\"></div>\n<div class=\"orbit-account account-left\"><div class=\"orb orb-a\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-intl.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance international app icon\"></div><div class=\"glass-account\"><span class=\"micro\">PLATFORM A</span><strong>Binance international</strong><span class=\"uid\">UID · 123456</span><span class=\"identity\"><i></i> Separate account system</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"orbit-account account-right\"><div class=\"orb orb-b\"><img class=\"platform-app-icon\" src=\"assets/teach/binance-th.png?v=20261108130000\" alt=\"Binance TH app icon\"></div><div class=\"glass-account\"><span class=\"micro\">PLATFORM B</span><strong>Binance TH</strong><span class=\"uid\">UID · 123456</span><span class=\"identity\"><i></i> Separate account system</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"broken-route\"><span class=\"route-line\"></span><div class=\"route-lock\"><span class=\"lock-symbol\">✕</span><b>Same UID is not the same recipient</b><small>Across platforms, a number alone does not identify the person</small></div><span class=\"route-line\"></span></div>\n<div class=\"orbit-foot\"><div><span class=\"tiny-dot\"></span> Check identity inside each platform</div><div><span class=\"tiny-dot\"></span> Confirm the transfer route the platform supports</div></div>\n</div><p class=\"diagram-caption\">The sample number only explains that the accounts are separate. It is not a real user's UID or a real transfer result.</p></div>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"num\">01 / PLATFORM</span><b>Look at the platform first</b><p>Are the sender and the receiver on the same system?</p></div><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"num\">02 / IDENTITY</span><b>Then look at the recipient</b><p>Do the UID, the username, and the avatar match what you expect?</p></div><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"num\">03 / ROUTE</span><b>Then look at the route</b><p>Does the platform explicitly support this kind of transfer?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>The core:</strong> a correct UID only means you checked one number. It does not prove the transfer route is right.</div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"c\" data-ok=\"Correct. Matching UID digits only means the number matches. You still have to confirm that both sides are the same system, and that the platform supports this way of receiving.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Matching digits, or an avatar that looks similar, cannot replace confirming the platform and the receiving method the official side supports.\"><h3>How would you judge this?</h3><p>The receiving UID matches the number you were sent, but you are not sure the two apps connect.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send it. The number is right</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Only check whether the avatar looks right</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. Stop and confirm the platform and the receiving method it supports</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "A UID is only a user number inside one platform, not a receiving identity for the whole industry. The same number does not mean the same recipient PLATFORM A Binance international UID · 123456 Separate account system PLATFORM B Binance TH UID · 123456 Separate account system ✕ Same UID is not the same recipient Across platforms, a number alone does not identify the person Check identity inside each platform Confirm the transfer route the platform supports The sample number only explains that the accounts are separate. It is not a real user's UID or a real transfer result. 01 / PLATFORM Look at the platform first Are the sender and the receiver on the same system? 02 / IDENTITY Then look at the recipient Do the UID, the username, and the avatar match what you expect? 03 / ROUTE Then look at the route Does the platform explicitly support this kind of transfer? The core: a correct UID only means you checked one number. It does not prove the transfer route is right. How would you judge this? The receiving UID matches the number you were sent, but you are not sure the two apps connect. A. Send it. The number is right B. Only check whether the avatar looks right C. Stop and confirm the platform and the receiving method it supports"}, {"title": "Before you transfer, get used to a three-step check", "html": "<p><strong>Before a transfer, confirm the platform, then the receiving details and the network; the first time you send somewhere, test a small amount before a large one.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>The safety check before a transfer</h3>\n<div class=\"cosmic-scene check-scene\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three stages: confirm the platform, check the receiving details and the network, then test a small amount\"><div class=\"check-track\"><div class=\"track-line\"></div>\n<div class=\"check-node\"><div class=\"node-orb orb-blue\"><span class=\"planet-texture\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"orb-number\">01</span></div><b>Confirm the platform</b><small>Does it support the transfer</small></div>\n<div class=\"check-node\"><div class=\"node-orb orb-rose\"><span class=\"planet-texture\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"orb-number\">02</span></div><b>Check the details</b><small>Identity · coin · network</small></div>\n<div class=\"check-node\"><div class=\"node-orb orb-gold\"><span class=\"planet-texture\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"orb-number\">03</span></div><b>Small test</b><small>Confirm it actually arrived</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"scene-bottom\">Finish the checks in order before you consider the real transfer</div></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: confirm the platform</strong></p>\n<p>See clearly which platform the sender belongs to, and which platform the receiver belongs to.</p>\n<p>In general, different app entrances correspond to different account systems and databases, especially platforms run for different countries or regions.</p>\n<p>So do not recognize only the brand logo.<br />\nEven if the names are similar, confirm the specific platform, whether the accounts connect, and whether an internal transfer is supported.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: confirm the receiving details and the network</strong></p>\n<p>For an internal transfer, besides the UID, the email, and other receiving identifiers, also check whether the <strong>username and avatar</strong> shown for the recipient match what you expect.</p>\n<p>But a username and an avatar are only extra checks. They cannot by themselves prove who the recipient is.</p>\n<p>For an on-chain transfer, check the coin, the receiving address, and the blockchain network.</p>\n<p>For example, USDT on TRC20 and USDT on ERC20 are not networks you can mix freely.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: test a small amount, then send the large one</strong></p>\n<p>Especially the first time you transfer to a platform, an address, or an account, test with a small amount first.</p>\n<p>Confirm that the other side actually received it, and that the coin and the network are right, before you send the rest.</p>\n<p>A small test cannot guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe, but it can at least stop one mistake from losing the whole amount.</p>\n<div class=\"checklist\"><label class=\"checkrow\"><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><b>Confirm the platform</b><small>Do not look only at the brand logo. Check the platform name and whether it supports this transfer.</small></span></label><label class=\"checkrow\"><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><b>Confirm the receiving details and the network</b><small>Internally, check the UID, username, avatar, and so on. On-chain, check the address, the coin, and the network. The avatar is only an extra check.</small></span></label><label class=\"checkrow\"><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><b>Test a small amount first</b><small>The first transfer to a place should be a small test. Confirm it really arrived before you consider the rest.</small></span></label></div>\n<p class=\"teach-status\" data-tpl=\"Checks {n} / {m} · scenario {quiz}\" data-yes=\"Passed\" data-no=\"Not yet\">Checks 0 / 3 · scenario Not yet</p>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Even after the UID, the username, and the avatar match, you still have to confirm that the two apps can transfer directly. If you are not sure, pause first.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Do not send a large amount as a test, and do not look only at the avatar. If you are not sure they connect, stop and check the method the official side supports.\"><h3>Check · scenario</h3><p>You have checked the UID, the username, and the avatar, and you are not sure the two apps can transfer directly. What should you do?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Send a large amount first, and see whether it arrives</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. Pause the transfer, and check the transfer method the official side supports</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"c\">C. If the avatars match, you can send</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>Note:</strong> a small test does not replace checking the platform and the recipient, and it does not guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe.</div>", "text": "Before a transfer, confirm the platform, then the receiving details and the network; the first time you send somewhere, test a small amount before a large one. The safety check before a transfer 01 Confirm the platform Does it support the transfer 02 Check the details Identity · coin · network 03 Small test Confirm it actually arrived Finish the checks in order before you consider the real transfer Step 1: confirm the platform See clearly which platform the sender belongs to, and which platform the receiver belongs to. In general, different app entrances correspond to different account systems and databases, especially platforms run for different countries or regions. So do not recognize only the brand logo. Even if the names are similar, confirm the specific platform, whether the accounts connect, and whether an internal transfer is supported. Step 2: confirm the receiving details and the network For an internal transfer, besides the UID, the email, and other receiving identifiers, also check whether the username and avatar shown for the recipient match what you expect. But a username and an avatar are only extra checks. They cannot by themselves prove who the recipient is. For an on-chain transfer, check the coin, the receiving address, and the blockchain network. For example, USDT on TRC20 and USDT on ERC20 are not networks you can mix freely. Step 3: test a small amount, then send the large one Especially the first time you transfer to a platform, an address, or an account, test with a small amount first. Confirm that the other side actually received it, and that the coin and the network are right, before you send the rest. A small test cannot guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe, but it can at least stop one mistake from losing the whole amount. Confirm the platform Do not look only at the brand logo. Check the platform name and whether it supports this transfer. Confirm the receiving details and the network Internally, check the UID, username, avatar, and so on. On-chain, check the address, the coin, and the network. The avatar is only an extra check. Test a small amount first The first transfer to a place should be a small test. Confirm it really arrived before you consider the rest. Checks 0 / 3 · scenario Not yet Check · scenario You have checked the UID, the username, and the avatar, and you are not sure the two apps can transfer directly. What should you do? A. Send a large amount first, and see whether it arrives B. Pause the transfer, and check the transfer method the official side supports C. If the avatars match, you can send Note: a small test does not replace checking the platform and the recipient, and it does not guarantee that later transfers are absolutely safe."}, {"title": "What if you already sent it to the wrong place?", "html": "<p><strong>After a wrong transfer, save the record and contact official support, but do not put your hope in fixing it afterward.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><h3>A wrong internal transfer: which steps usually come next?</h3>\n<div class=\"cosmic-scene recovery-scene\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Steps after a wrong internal transfer: keep evidence, an official appeal, the platform checks, talk with the recipient, handle it under the rules\"><div class=\"recovery-track\"><div class=\"track-line\"></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">01</div><b>Keep evidence</b><small>Records and screenshots</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">02</div><b>Official appeal</b><small>Submit the details</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">03</div><b>Platform check</b><small>Verify the trade</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">04</div><b>Talk it through</b><small>Contact the recipient</small></div>\n<div class=\"recovery-node\"><div class=\"recovery-orb\">05</div><b>Follow the rules</b><small>The result is not guaranteed</small></div>\n</div><div class=\"scene-bottom\">The platform cannot move another user's assets on one person's word alone</div></div></div>\n\n<p>First save the transfer record, the order number, the receiving UID, the transaction hash if there is one, and the related screenshots, and contact official support as soon as you can.</p>\n<p>If it was a wrong internal transfer, you usually have to file an appeal or a report under the platform's rules. The platform checks the transaction record and, depending on the case, helps contact the actual recipient to talk it through.</p>\n<p>One basic principle:</p>\n<p><strong>Once the funds are in another user's account, the exchange cannot take them back out of that account on the sender's word alone.</strong></p>\n<p>Even if the transfer really was a mistake, it still has to go through identity checks, an investigation of the transaction, and the matching process.</p>\n<p>If the other side will not cooperate, recovering the funds can be very hard, and you may need a legal route.</p>\n<p>If it was a wrong on-chain transfer, a transaction that is already confirmed usually cannot be undone.<br />\nIn particular, if it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, the chance of recovery can be very small.</p>\n<p>So do not put your hope in fixing it afterward.</p>\n<p>A last reminder</p>\n<p>The easiest place for a crypto transfer to go wrong is sometimes not a complicated technique. It is a piece of common sense you thought was obvious.</p>\n<p>Two extra minutes of checking before you send is much easier than several days of looking for a fix after it is wrong.</p>\n<div class=\"compare\"><div><b>Wrong internal transfer</b><p>The platform can check the record and handle it under the rules, but it cannot take another user's assets on the sender's word alone. Another person's account usually needs verification, contact, and the proper process.</p></div><div><b>Wrong on-chain transfer</b><p>A transaction that is confirmed on-chain usually cannot be undone. If it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, recovery can be extremely hard, or impossible.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"key\"><strong>A plain reminder:</strong> after a wrong transfer there is no one-tap undo. What you can do is use the formal channel quickly. Fixing it afterward is not a safety net.</div>\n<div class=\"quiz\" data-tone=\"box\" data-correct=\"b\" data-ok=\"Correct. Once the money is in someone else's account, the exchange cannot pull it back just because you described what happened. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the process.\" data-bad=\"Not quite. Explaining that it was a mistake does not mean the platform can pull the funds straight back from the other account. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the process.\"><h3>If the funds are already in someone else's account?</h3><p>Can the exchange pull the money back from the other account on your description alone?</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"a\">A. Yes, as long as you explain it was a mistake</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"option\" data-answer=\"b\">B. No. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the relevant process</button><div class=\"feedback\"></div></div>", "text": "After a wrong transfer, save the record and contact official support, but do not put your hope in fixing it afterward. A wrong internal transfer: which steps usually come next? 01 Keep evidence Records and screenshots 02 Official appeal Submit the details 03 Platform check Verify the trade 04 Talk it through Contact the recipient 05 Follow the rules The result is not guaranteed The platform cannot move another user's assets on one person's word alone First save the transfer record, the order number, the receiving UID, the transaction hash if there is one, and the related screenshots, and contact official support as soon as you can. If it was a wrong internal transfer, you usually have to file an appeal or a report under the platform's rules. The platform checks the transaction record and, depending on the case, helps contact the actual recipient to talk it through. One basic principle: Once the funds are in another user's account, the exchange cannot take them back out of that account on the sender's word alone. Even if the transfer really was a mistake, it still has to go through identity checks, an investigation of the transaction, and the matching process. If the other side will not cooperate, recovering the funds can be very hard, and you may need a legal route. If it was a wrong on-chain transfer, a transaction that is already confirmed usually cannot be undone. In particular, if it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, the chance of recovery can be very small. So do not put your hope in fixing it afterward. A last reminder The easiest place for a crypto transfer to go wrong is sometimes not a complicated technique. It is a piece of common sense you thought was obvious. Two extra minutes of checking before you send is much easier than several days of looking for a fix after it is wrong. Wrong internal transfer The platform can check the record and handle it under the rules, but it cannot take another user's assets on the sender's word alone. Another person's account usually needs verification, contact, and the proper process. Wrong on-chain transfer A transaction that is confirmed on-chain usually cannot be undone. If it went to an address you cannot control, or onto the wrong network, recovery can be extremely hard, or impossible. A plain reminder: after a wrong transfer there is no one-tap undo. What you can do is use the formal channel quickly. Fixing it afterward is not a safety net. If the funds are already in someone else's account? Can the exchange pull the money back from the other account on your description alone? A. Yes, as long as you explain it was a mistake B. No. It has to be checked, and it has to follow the relevant process"}]}]};
